window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("delivery-ownership", {"topics":[{"key":"delivery-ownership/production-debugging","sources":["David J. Agans, 2002 — Debugging: The 9 Indispensable Rules","Beyer, Jones, Petoff, Murphy (eds.), 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering"],"why":"- **You cannot attach a debugger to yesterday.** In development you control the input, code version and\n  data and can step through it. In production you get a ticket: \"the assistant told a customer the wrong\n  refund policy yesterday afternoon.\"\n- **The evidence has moved on.** The request is gone, the process that served it has been recycled, the\n  vector index may have been rebuilt since, and the provider may have updated the model behind the API.\n- **The naive replay lies.** Pasting the user's question into a notebook almost always \"works\", so the\n  ticket closes as \"cannot reproduce\". The question is a small fraction of what the model received: a\n  system prompt at some template version, chunks from the index *as it was then*, history, tool outputs,\n  a specific model snapshot and temperature. Change any of these and you debug a different request.\n- **Sampling hides failures.** A failure that happens 1 time in 10 looks fixed in a single replay 90% of\n  the time.\n- **What the discipline buys.** Capture enough at request time to reconstruct the failure later, then\n  narrow it by evidence rather than by guessing and redeploying.","how":"### 1. Stabilize before you diagnose\n- **Mitigate first if users are harmed now.** Roll back the last prompt or model change, flip a feature\n  flag, route to a fallback, or disable the failing tool.\n- **Why before the cause is known:** diagnosis can take hours while damage accrues per request.\n- **Capture evidence before rolling back** (trace IDs, a sample of bad outputs, current config), because\n  the rollback destroys the live state you would otherwise examine.\n\n### 2. Scope the failure\n- **Ask: since when, for whom, how often?** Query metrics and logs for a failure rate over time, sliced by\n  tenant, model, prompt version, region and client version.\n- **Read the shape:**\n\n| Pattern | Points at |\n|---|---|\n| Step change at 14:02 | An event (deploy, flag, rebuild) |\n| Slow climb | Drift: index staleness, traffic mix |\n| Confined to one tenant | Their data or config, not your code |\n\n- **Output of this step:** \"it's broken\" becomes \"3% of tenant X's requests since the 14:00 deploy.\"\n\n### 3. Find concrete failing requests\n- **Pick specific failing requests** by correlation or trace ID, plus a few *succeeding* requests from the\n  same window.\n- **Diff bad against good** (differential debugging). It is usually faster than studying the bad trace\n  alone, because the difference is the clue.\n\n### 4. Reconstruct the exact input\nRecover everything that determined the output:\n- **the fully rendered messages**, not the template plus variables;\n- **the model identifier and snapshot the API actually reported**, not the alias you configured;\n- **sampling and format:** temperature, top_p, max tokens, tool schemas, response format;\n- **retrieved chunk IDs and their text** *at that time*;\n- **every tool call and its raw result**, including errors and truncation.\n\nIf any of these was not logged, that gap is itself a finding: fixing logging becomes an action item.\n\n### 5. Bisect the pipeline\nAn LLM application is a chain: input handling → retrieval → prompt assembly → model call → parsing →\ntool execution → post-processing. Check the intermediate output at each boundary.\n\n| What the trace shows | Where the bug is |\n|---|---|\n| Right document never retrieved | Retrieval (no prompt change will help) |\n| Retrieved but below a truncation limit | Prompt assembly |\n| In the prompt, model ignored it | Generation |\n\nBisecting is logarithmic in the number of stages, which is why it beats reading the whole trace linearly.\n\n### 5b. Bisect an agent run by step\nAn agent repeats that chain once per step, so a run is a sequence of (model decision, tool call, tool\nresult) triples. Find the **first divergent step**, because every later step inherits its error.\n\n| What step k shows | Where the bug is |\n|---|---|\n| Tool result wrong or truncated, decision reasonable | The tool or its integration |\n| Tool result fine, model picked the wrong tool or arguments | Tool descriptions, prompt or model |\n| Loop never stops, same call repeated | Stop condition, step budget or error handling |\n| Early steps fine, later steps forget facts | Memory or context compaction |\n\n- **Compare against a good run** of the same task: the step where the two transcripts first differ is\n  where to look.\n- **Replay from step k**, not from step 1, by feeding recorded tool results for steps before k, so the\n  earlier, already-correct steps cannot drift and hide the bug.\n\n### 6. Replay with one variable changed, many times\n1. **Measure the baseline.** Replay the reconstructed input N times at the logged parameters to get a\n   failure rate.\n2. **Change exactly one factor** (current index instead of historical, new prompt version, other model)\n   and measure again.\n3. **Why repeat:** a single stochastic sample proves nothing.\n4. **Why one factor:** changing two leaves you unable to say which one fixed it.\n\n- **Temperature 0 is not determinism on a hosted model.** Batching and floating-point effects on the\n  provider side can still vary outputs, and seed parameters, where offered, are typically best-effort.\n  Even a \"deterministic\" replay deserves a handful of repeats before you trust it.\n- **Stub side-effecting tools.** Feed the model the recorded tool results instead of calling live tools,\n  *because* a replay that re-executes `issue_refund` is a second incident, and because live tools return\n  today's data, which silently changes the input you were trying to hold fixed.\n\n### When the failure is latency, cost or errors rather than a wrong answer\nThe same scope-trace-bisect loop applies, but the evidence is the span waterfall rather than the content.\n- **Find the critical path** in a slow trace. Is the time in queueing before the model call, the\n  provider's time to first token, a long generation, retries, or a tool?\n- **Output length often dominates end-to-end latency**, because each output token is a separate decode\n  step.\n- **Compare p50 against p99.** A healthy median with a terrible tail usually means a minority of requests\n  do something different: retry storms, oversized contexts, one slow tool, agent loops that ran many more\n  steps. Find what distinguishes them; do not speed up the average request.\n- **Cost spikes yield to the same slicing.** Tokens per request by tenant, route and prompt version\n  usually reveal one path whose context or step count grew.\n\n### 7. Correlate with change\n- **Most production regressions follow a change:** a deploy, prompt edit, config or flag flip, index\n  rebuild, dependency upgrade, upstream schema change, or a provider-side model update.\n- **Keep a timestamped change log** so you can line up the onset of the failure against candidate causes.\n\n### 8. Close the loop\n- **Ship the fix with the captured failing case added to the regression suite**, so the same bug cannot\n  silently return.\n- **Close the logging gaps found in step 4** in the same change.","example":"### Replay harness: baseline, then one factor at a time\nA minimal replay harness. It loads a logged trace, replays it at the original parameters to estimate the\nfailure rate, then swaps one factor. `complete` stands in for your provider client and `check` for a\ntask-specific assertion.\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom statistics import mean\n\ndef complete(messages, model, **params) -> str:\n    raise NotImplementedError(\"call your provider SDK here\")\n\ndef check(output: str) -> bool:\n    # task-specific: did the answer state the 30-day refund window?\n    return \"30 days\" in output\n\ndef replay(trace, n=20, **overrides):\n    t = {**trace, **overrides}            # override exactly one factor\n    outs = [complete(t[\"messages\"], t[\"model\"], **t[\"params\"]) for _ in range(n)]\n    return mean(not check(o) for o in outs)   # failure rate\n\ntrace = json.load(open(\"trace_7f3a.json\"))   # rendered messages, model, params, chunk ids\nbaseline = replay(trace)\nprint(f\"failure rate at logged inputs: {baseline:.0%}\")\n\n# Hypothesis: the retrieved chunk was the old policy page. Rebuild messages with today's chunk.\nfixed_ctx = dict(trace, messages=rebuild_with_current_chunks(trace))  # your helper\nprint(f\"with current index: {replay(fixed_ctx):.0%}\")\n\n# Hypothesis: temperature is the cause. Change only that.\nprint(f\"at temperature 0: {replay(trace, params={**trace['params'], 'temperature': 0}):.0%}\")\n```\n\n- **Reading the result:** if the baseline reproduces at, say, 40% and swapping in today's chunk drops it\n  to 0% while temperature 0 still fails, the defect is stale retrieval, not sampling. The fix belongs in\n  index freshness.\n- **Keep the sample size honest:** 0 failures in 20 replays only bounds the true rate below roughly 3/20\n  ≈ 15% at 95% confidence (the \"rule of three\"). A failure that was rare to begin with needs a few hundred\n  replays before \"0%\" means fixed.\n\n### How many replays prove a fix (runnable)\nPure Python, no dependencies. The exact bound for zero observed failures is `1 - 0.05 ** (1/n)`; the rule\nof three approximates it as `3/n`.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\ndef zero_failure_upper_bound(n, conf=0.95):\n    \"\"\"Highest true failure rate still consistent with 0 failures in n replays.\"\"\"\n    return 1 - (1 - conf) ** (1 / n)\n\ndef replays_needed(target_rate, conf=0.95):\n    \"\"\"Replays with 0 failures needed to claim the rate is below target_rate.\"\"\"\n    return math.ceil(math.log(1 - conf) / math.log(1 - target_rate))\n\nfor n in (20, 100, 300):\n    print(n, f\"exact {zero_failure_upper_bound(n):.1%}\", f\"rule of three {3 / n:.1%}\")\n# 20 exact 13.9% rule of three 15.0%\n# 100 exact 3.0% rule of three 3.0%\n# 300 exact 1.0% rule of three 1.0%\nprint(replays_needed(0.10), replays_needed(0.01))   # 29 299\n```\n\n### Agent replay from the first bad step, with tools stubbed\nReplays step `k` of a recorded agent run. Earlier steps and every tool call are served from the trace,\nso nothing live is executed and only the decision at step `k` is sampled again. `complete` is your\nprovider call; it returns the model's next tool call (or final answer) as a dict.\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef complete(messages, model, tools, **params) -> dict:\n    raise NotImplementedError(\"call your provider SDK here\")\n\ndef messages_before(run, k):\n    \"\"\"Rebuild the exact transcript the model saw before step k from recorded steps.\"\"\"\n    msgs = list(run[\"initial_messages\"])\n    for step in run[\"steps\"][:k]:\n        msgs.append(step[\"assistant_message\"])           # recorded tool call\n        msgs.append({\"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": step[\"call_id\"],\n                     \"content\": step[\"raw_tool_result\"]})  # recorded result, never re-executed\n    return msgs\n\ndef replay_step(run, k, n=20, **overrides):   # k is 0-based: steps[:k] are served from the trace\n    t = {**run, **overrides}                              # one factor at a time\n    msgs = messages_before(t, k)\n    picks = [complete(msgs, t[\"model\"], t[\"tools\"], **t[\"params\"]) for _ in range(n)]\n    return Counter(p.get(\"tool_name\", \"final_answer\") for p in picks)\n\nrun = json.load(open(\"run_4c19.json\"))   # rendered messages, model snapshot, params, steps\nprint(replay_step(run, k=3))             # e.g. Counter({'cancel_order': 9, 'lookup_order': 11})\n```\n\n- **Reading the result:** if step 3 picks `cancel_order` 9 times in 20 at the logged inputs, the bad\n  decision reproduces at that step; now change only the tool descriptions, or only the model, and count\n  again.\n\n### Worked scenario: an agent loop that ran 41 steps\nA support agent normally finishes in about 6 steps. After a release, cost per conversation tripled\n(numbers illustrative).\n\n| Step | Evidence | Conclusion |\n|---|---|---|\n| Scope | p50 steps 6, p99 steps 41 since Tuesday's deploy | A minority path, an event |\n| Trace diff | Long runs call `search_orders` again and again | Loop at the tool step |\n| First divergent step | Step 4: the tool returns an error string, the model retries with the same arguments | Error handling |\n| Change log | The deploy renamed a field the tool expects | Candidate cause |\n| Replay step 4, n=20, tools stubbed | Same retry chosen 20 of 20 | Reproduced on every sample |\n| Fix | Restore the field, return a structured error, cap identical calls per run, add the run as a regression case | Closed |\n\n### Worked incident: wrong refund window\n| Step | Evidence | Conclusion |\n|---|---|---|\n| Scope | 3% of tenant X's refund questions wrong since 14:02 | Event, not drift |\n| Change log | Index rebuild at 14:00, no code deploy | Candidate cause |\n| Trace diff | Bad traces retrieved the archived policy page, good ones the current page | Retrieval stage |\n| Baseline replay, n=20 | 8 of 20 fail (40%) | Reproduced |\n| Swap in current chunk, n=300 | 0 of 300 fail | Below ~1% at 95% confidence |\n| Fix | Exclude archived pages in the index filter, add the trace as a regression case | Closed |","patterns":["**Trace ID through every stage.** When: from day one. How: generate one ID at the edge, propagate it into retrieval, model, tool and parsing spans (for example as an OpenTelemetry trace), and print it in user-facing error messages. Monitor: share of requests with a complete trace.","**Log the resolved model, not the alias.** When: any hosted model call. How: store the model field the provider's response reports next to the alias you requested. Monitor: alert when the resolved snapshot changes without a deploy on your side.","**Record-and-stub replay.** When: tools have side effects or return time-varying data. How: persist each tool call's raw result in the trace and have the replay harness serve those results instead of calling live tools. Monitor: replays that accidentally hit a live tool (should be zero).","**Sampled full-payload capture with redaction.** When: volume or PII makes logging everything costly. How: keep full rendered prompts for a fixed sample plus every errored or flagged request, redact before storage, and set a retention window. Monitor: time-to-reproduce and storage per day.","**One span per agent step.** When: any tool-calling loop. How: emit a child span for each model call and each tool call under the run's root span, with the step number, tool name, arguments and a hash or copy of the raw result. Monitor: steps per run (p50 and p99) and identical repeated tool calls per run.","**Change log joined to dashboards.** When: always. How: emit an annotation event for every deploy, prompt edit, flag flip and index rebuild onto the same time axis as error and quality metrics. Monitor: fraction of incidents whose onset lines up with a logged change."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent run is many model calls, tool calls and retrievals chained\n  together, so \"it gave a wrong answer\" can start at any step. Without a trace you cannot tell a bad tool\n  result at step 3 from a bad decision at step 7.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Tracing spans per model call and tool call (OpenTelemetry or your\n  framework's tracer), the run or conversation ID, recorded tool results, the resolved model snapshot,\n  and a replay harness that can restart a run from any step.\n- **What you control.** What every step logs (rendered messages, tool arguments, raw tool results),\n  whether side-effecting tools can be stubbed in replay, and whether one ID joins every span of a run.\n- **What you can skip.** Classic debugger tooling and profilers for the model itself; the leverage for\n  agents is in traces, replay and bisecting the steps of a run.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Support agents giving wrong policy answers.** Trace the run, check whether retrieval returned the\n  current page, and replay with the historical context to confirm.\n- **Coding and ops agents that loop.** Steps-per-run p99 spikes; the first divergent step usually shows a\n  tool error the model keeps retrying.\n- **Agents that took a harmful action.** Replay the decision step with tools stubbed so the investigation\n  never re-issues the refund, email or deletion.\n- **Silent provider model updates.** The resolved snapshot in the trace changes with no deploy on your\n  side, and replays at old and new snapshots isolate the cause.","mini_project":"**Build a step-level replay harness for a small agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** turn any recorded agent run into a reproducible test that restarts from a chosen step.\n- **Steps:** build a 3-tool agent (one tool side-effecting, such as a fake `send_email`); log every step\n  as a JSON record with rendered messages, tool name, arguments and raw result; write `replay_step(run, k,\n  n)` that serves recorded results for earlier steps and stubs every tool; inject a bug (a tool that\n  returns a truncated result) and use the harness to locate it.\n- **Done when:** the harness reports the first divergent step and a failure rate over 20 replays, never\n  calls a live tool, and the failing run is saved as a regression case that passes after the fix."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"User report or alert\"] --> H{\"Users harmed now?\"}\n    H -->|\"yes\"| E[\"Snapshot traces and config\"]\n    E --> M[\"Roll back or flag off\"]\n    H -->|\"no\"| S[\"Scope: since when, who, how often\"]\n    M --> S\n    S --> T[\"Pull failing and good traces\"]\n    T --> X[\"Reconstruct exact input\"]\n    X --> B[\"Bisect stages and agent steps\"]\n    B --> P[\"Replay N times, change one factor\"]\n    P -->|\"rate unchanged\"| B\n    P -->|\"rate drops\"| F[\"Fix plus regression case\"]","caption":"Mitigation comes before diagnosis; the replay loop changes one factor at a time until the stage, or\nthe first bad agent step, is found."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is \"I pasted the question into the playground and it worked\" not a reproduction?","a":"- **The question is a fraction of the input.** The model also saw a versioned system prompt, retrieved\n  chunks as the index was then, history, tool results, a model snapshot and sampling parameters.\n- **So the playground tests a different request.** A real reproduction rebuilds the rendered messages\n  from the trace and replays them at the logged parameters."},{"q":"Latency p50 is fine but p99 doubled after a release. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Slice the tail, not the average.** Pull the slowest traces and compare their span waterfalls with\n  median traces from the same window.\n- **Look for what distinguishes them:** retry storms, oversized contexts, one slow tool, longer outputs, or\n  agent loops that ran more steps.\n- **Line the onset up with the change log**, then fix the minority path rather than tuning the common one."},{"q":"When would you not roll back immediately?","a":"- **When nobody is being harmed now.** A cosmetic or low-rate issue can be diagnosed live, keeping the\n  evidence in place.\n- **When rollback is itself risky**, for example a schema or index change that cannot be reverted\n  cleanly; then a flag, fallback route or disabling one tool may be the safer mitigation.\n- **Otherwise roll back**, after snapshotting traces and config, because harm accrues per request."},{"q":"You replayed a fix 20 times with no failures. Is it fixed?","a":"- **Not necessarily.** Zero in 20 only bounds the true rate below about 15% at 95% confidence (rule of\n  three, 3/n).\n- **Size the replay to the original rate.** A 1% failure needs roughly 300 clean replays before \"0%\"\n  means fixed."},{"q":"An agent took a wrong action at step 7 of 12. How do you find out why?","a":"- **Find the first divergent step.** Compare the run's transcript with a good run of the same task; the\n  error often starts earlier than the visible action, for example a truncated tool result at step 5.\n- **Replay from that step with tools stubbed.** Rebuild the transcript from recorded steps, sample the\n  decision 20 or more times, then change one factor: tool description, model, or the tool result itself.\n- **Close it** with the run added to the agent's regression suite."},{"q":"What must a trace contain for an agent run to be debuggable later?","a":"- **Rendered messages** and the resolved model snapshot, not the template or alias.\n- **Sampling and format parameters**, tool schemas, retrieved chunk IDs with their text at the time, and\n  every raw tool result including errors.\n- **One run ID shared across stages and one span per step**, so retrieval, model and tool spans can be\n  joined in order.\n- **Record-and-stub replay** so any run can be restarted from any step without side effects, plus a\n  change log on the same time axis as steps-per-run, tool error rate and cost per run."}],"tradeoffs":["**Full capture vs privacy and storage.** Logging full rendered prompts and tool results makes reconstruction possible, but costs storage and creates a PII store. It flips toward sampling or redaction when inputs are sensitive or volume is huge.","**Immediate rollback vs live evidence.** Rolling back stops harm but destroys the live state you wanted to inspect. It is the right call whenever users are affected, provided you snapshot evidence first.","**Many replays vs tokens and time.** Many samples give a trustworthy failure rate but cost tokens and time; for deterministic failures (parsing, tool errors) a single replay is enough.","**Production data vs safety.** Debugging directly against production data gives the most faithful reproduction but risks side effects and access violations. Prefer read-only replicas and replay with tools stubbed to recorded results."],"pitfalls":["**Replaying only the user's question.** Symptom: a long run of \"cannot reproduce\" closures for problems customers keep reporting. Fix: replay the full rendered input.","**Trusting a single successful replay of a stochastic failure.** Symptom: a fix that \"worked\" in testing and a production failure rate that never moved. Fix: measure rates over many replays.","**Logging the configured model alias.** Symptom: a regression nobody can explain because no deploy happened on your side. Fix: log the snapshot the API returned.","**Changing several things in one hotfix.** Symptom: the problem goes away and nobody can say why, so it returns on the next unrelated change. Fix: one factor per change.","**Debugging before mitigating.** Symptom: an hour of elegant diagnosis while the incident's impact keeps growing. Fix: snapshot, roll back, then diagnose.","**Replaying with live tools.** Symptom: duplicate side effects (a second email or refund) or replays whose tool outputs differ from the trace, so the \"reproduction\" is not one. Fix: stub to recorded results.","**Replaying an agent from step 1 to debug step 7.** Symptom: earlier steps take a different path each replay, so the failure seems to vanish. Fix: replay from the first divergent step with earlier tool results served from the trace."],"when":"- **Use when:** a live agent or LLM system produces wrong, slow, looping or failed results and the cause\n  is not obvious from one error message.\n- **Invest upfront:** trace IDs threaded through every stage, rendered prompts and retrieval results\n  captured (or sampled), a change log, and a replay harness. This is the investment that pays for itself.\n- **Measure:** time-to-reproduce. If most investigations stall at \"cannot reproduce\", the problem is\n  instrumentation, not skill.\n- **Interviews:** a strong answer to \"a customer says the bot gave a wrong answer\" walks through mitigate,\n  scope, find the trace, reconstruct, bisect, replay and regress, rather than jumping to \"I'd tweak the\n  prompt.\"","role_lens":{"engineer":"Instruments every stage with trace IDs, records rendered prompts and model snapshots, and owns the replay harness that turns tickets into reproducible test cases.","architect":"Decides what the platform must capture by default, how long it is retained, who can access it, and where redaction happens, so debugging is possible without a privacy breach.","fde":"Debugs inside a customer environment with limited access, so negotiates log export, read-only replicas and trace sharing early, before the first production issue arrives."},"check":{"q":"A customer reports the assistant cited a discontinued product twice last week. You paste the\nquestion into the playground and get a correct answer. What do you do next, and why is your playground\nresult not evidence?","a":"The playground used today's prompt template, today's index and possibly a different model\nsnapshot and temperature, so it tested a different request. I would find the two traces by request ID,\nrecover the rendered messages, retrieved chunk IDs and model snapshot, and check the retrieval stage\nfirst: was the discontinued product's page returned? If so, the index or its filters are stale and the\nfix is freshness, not the prompt. I would replay the logged input 20 or more times to get a failure rate,\nthen swap only the retrieved context to confirm the cause, and add the case to the regression suite."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should you replay a reconstructed LLM request many times rather than once?","options":["Providers rate-limit single requests more aggressively than batches","Sampling makes the output stochastic, so one replay cannot distinguish a fixed bug from a lucky draw","The first call warms the cache, so only later replays reflect production behaviour","Multiple replays are required to recover the original random seed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With temperature above zero a failure may occur in only a fraction of samples; measuring the failure rate across many replays, before and after a change, is the only way to show the change mattered."},{"q":"An answer was wrong. The trace shows the correct document was the top retrieval result, but the prompt assembler's token budget cut it before the model call. Where is the defect?","options":["In the embedding model, which ranked the document too low","In the generation step, which should have inferred the missing content","In prompt assembly, which dropped retrieved content before it reached the model","In sampling, because a lower temperature would have found it"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Bisecting the pipeline shows retrieval did its job and the model never saw the content; the fix belongs in how context is budgeted and ordered, not in retrieval tuning or prompt wording."},{"q":"Users are receiving harmful outputs after a prompt change an hour ago. What comes first?","options":["Reproduce the failure locally so the rollback can be justified","Write the regression test so the bug cannot recur","Bisect the pipeline to locate the faulty stage","Capture sample trace IDs and config, then roll back the prompt change"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Mitigation precedes diagnosis when users are being harmed, but grabbing evidence first keeps the investigation possible after the rollback erases the live state."}]},{"key":"delivery-ownership/failure-analysis","sources":[],"why":"- **Debugging explains one failure; products fail in hundreds of ways a day.** An answer misses a detail,\n  a tool call gets a wrong argument, a refusal should not have happened, a request times out.\n- **Reacting to reports funds the loudest failure.** Effort goes to the angry enterprise customer, the\n  social-media screenshot, the bug the CEO hit. The loudest is rarely the most common, and fixing one at a\n  time leaves the dominant modes untouched.\n- **Aggregate metrics say \"wrong\", not \"what\".** \"Satisfaction dropped from 82% to 76%\" tells you\n  something broke, not what to change.\n- **Big swaps are guesses.** A bigger model or a full prompt rewrite costs money and often moves the error\n  elsewhere.\n- **Classifiers first bake in imagined categories.** Building one before anyone has read the data encodes\n  what the team imagined, not what occurs. Failure analysis turns a pile of failures into a ranked,\n  quantified list of causes, so the next fix has the largest expected reduction in harm.","how":"### 1. Define what counts as a failure\n| Signal type | Examples | Bias |\n|---|---|---|\n| Explicit | Thumbs-down, escalation to a human, complaint | Over-represents engaged, annoyed users |\n| Implicit | User rephrases the same question, abandons the session, copies nothing | Noisy per case |\n| System | Exception, timeout, schema validation failure, tool error | Misses confident wrong answers |\n| Evaluation | An LLM judge or rule flags the output | Only as good as the judge |\n\n- **Use several sources**, because each is biased and combining them reduces the blind spots of any one.\n\n### 2. Sample without bias\n- **Random, not forwarded.** Pull a random sample of failing cases from a fixed window, stratified if\n  needed by tenant or use case, rather than the cases someone forwarded.\n- **A few hundred is usually enough** to rank the top modes, because you are estimating proportions, not\n  finding every bug.\n- **Pull some successes too.** They calibrate whether a \"failure pattern\" is really specific to failures.\n- **Label a small random sample of *all* traffic, flagged or not.** Failure signals miss the confident\n  wrong answer nobody thumbs down. This sample is the only way to estimate the silent failure rate.\n- **It also converts shares to rates.** It turns \"38% of flagged failures\" into an absolute rate per\n  conversation, which is what a fix's benefit is actually measured in.\n\n### 3. Open coding, then a taxonomy\n1. **Read each case end to end** (the trace, not just the final answer) and write a short free-text note\n   on the *first* thing that went wrong.\n2. **Anchor on the first error**, because later errors are often consequences of it. A wrong retrieval\n   causes a wrong citation causes a wrong answer: count one failure, not three.\n3. **Read until saturation.** Stop when new cases stop producing new kinds of note; that, not a fixed\n   quota, tells you the taxonomy is complete enough to rank.\n4. **Cluster the notes** into categories (\"retrieved outdated policy\", \"tool called with wrong date\n   format\", \"ignored instruction to ask for order ID\"), each tagged with its originating pipeline stage.\n5. **Double-code a few dozen cases.** If two coders disagree often, the definitions are ambiguous and the\n   counts that follow are noise.\n\nThis is the discipline the evals module calls error analysis, applied here to live production traffic.\n\n### Labelling agent trajectories\nFor a multi-step agent, the \"first error\" is the first bad **step**. Stages worth tagging:\n\n| Stage | Typical first error |\n|---|---|\n| Planning or tool choice | Picked the wrong tool, or skipped a required lookup |\n| Tool arguments | Wrong format, invented ID, missing field |\n| Tool result handling | Ignored an error string, trusted a truncated result |\n| Retrieval or memory | Missing or stale context, forgot an earlier fact |\n| Control flow | Looped, hit the step budget, stopped too early |\n| Policy | Acted when it should have asked or escalated |\n\n- **Record the step number too**, because failures clustered at step 1 point at prompts and tool\n  descriptions, while failures late in long runs point at context growth or memory.\n\n### 4. Count and weight\n- **Share with uncertainty.** A proportion *p* from *n* samples has standard error √(p(1−p)/n).\n- **Weight by severity**, because a rare failure that leaks another tenant's data outranks a common one\n  that adds an extra sentence.\n- **Simple score:** frequency × severity × reach (fraction of users or revenue exposed).\n\n### 5. Attribute and choose fixes\nFor each top category, estimate the most a fix could buy before choosing one:\n\n```\nexpected reduction in failure rate ≈ overall failure rate × category share × fix efficacy\n```\n\n- **Category share is a hard ceiling.** A perfect fix for a 12% category removes at most 12% of failures,\n  however impressive the intervention.\n- **Rank by** that reduction, weighted by severity, divided by the fix's cost.\n- **Pick the cheapest effective intervention at the originating stage**, because fixing there beats\n  patching downstream symptoms:\n\n| Category | Intervention |\n|---|---|\n| Stale retrieval | Index freshness |\n| Malformed tool arguments | A schema or validator |\n| Ignored instructions | A prompt rule or few-shot example |\n| Unsafe content | A guardrail |\n\n### 6. Make it repeatable\n- **Automate the top categories** as detectors or eval cases so their rate becomes a tracked metric.\n- **Repeat the manual pass periodically**, because new features and traffic shifts create new categories.\n\n### Reactive versus proactive\n- **This lesson is reactive:** it analyses failures that happened.\n- **FMEA is the proactive counterpart:** it enumerates failure modes before launch and scores severity,\n  occurrence and detectability.\n- **They feed each other**, since observed failures update the FMEA's occurrence estimates.","example":"### Worked example: the bigger-model instinct\nA support assistant handles about 10,000 conversations a week. The satisfaction metric has dropped and\nthe team's instinct is to switch to a larger model. Instead, they randomly sample 200 conversations that\nhad a failure signal and code the first error in each:\n\n| Category (originating stage)            | Count | Share |\n|-----------------------------------------|------:|------:|\n| Retrieved outdated policy (retrieval)   | 76    | 38%   |\n| Wrong order lookup argument (tool)      | 44    | 22%   |\n| Answered when it should escalate (policy)| 30   | 15%   |\n| Reasoning error with correct context (model) | 24 | 12% |\n| Timeout or provider error (infra)       | 16    | 8%    |\n| Other                                   | 10    | 5%    |\n\n- **Uncertainty:** the top share has standard error √(0.38 × 0.62 / 200) ≈ 0.034, so a 95% interval of\n  roughly 31%–45%: wide, but clearly the largest bucket.\n- **The model is a small target.** Only 12% of failures are genuine model reasoning errors, so a bigger\n  model directly targets about an eighth of the problem while increasing cost on every call.\n- **Spillover is limited.** It may help somewhat with tool arguments and escalation judgement, but it\n  cannot fix stale retrieval or provider timeouts, which together are 46% of failures.\n- **Stage fixes attack more.** Re-indexing on document change plus filtering superseded policies attacks\n  38%; validating the order-ID format before the tool call attacks another 22%.\n- **Severity reorders the list.** The escalation category is lower in frequency but higher in severity (a\n  customer given a wrong commitment), so it goes second in priority despite its share.\n\n**Same unit for every option.** Suppose (illustratively) that 8% of conversations fail.\n\n| Option | Calculation | Overall reduction |\n|---|---|---|\n| Freshness fix removing 80% of stale-retrieval failures | 0.08 × 0.38 × 0.8 | ≈ 2.4 percentage points |\n| Larger model fixing half of reasoning errors | 0.08 × 0.12 × 0.5 | ≈ 0.5 points, plus spillover on tool arguments, at a higher price per request |\n\nThe efficacy figures are guesses, which is fine: the category share bounds each option, and the next\nsample tells you whether the guess held.\n\n### Ranking script (runnable)\nPure Python. The severity scores are illustrative (1 = cosmetic, 4 = wrong commitment); replace them with\nthe scale your team agrees.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\ncounts = {\"stale retrieval\": 76, \"tool argument\": 44, \"missed escalation\": 30,\n          \"model reasoning\": 24, \"infra timeout\": 16, \"other\": 10}\nseverity = {\"stale retrieval\": 2, \"tool argument\": 2, \"missed escalation\": 4,\n            \"model reasoning\": 2, \"infra timeout\": 1, \"other\": 1}\nn = sum(counts.values())                 # 200\nfailure_rate = 0.08                      # illustrative: 8% of conversations fail\n\ndef share_ci(k, n, z=1.96):\n    p = k / n\n    se = math.sqrt(p * (1 - p) / n)\n    return p, max(0.0, p - z * se), p + z * se\n\nrows = []\nfor cat, k in counts.items():\n    p, lo, hi = share_ci(k, n)\n    rows.append((p * severity[cat], cat, p, lo, hi))\nfor score, cat, p, lo, hi in sorted(rows, reverse=True):\n    print(f\"{cat:18} share {p:4.0%} (95% CI {lo:.0%}-{hi:.0%})  weighted {score:.2f}\")\n# stale retrieval first (0.76), missed escalation second (0.60), tool argument third (0.44)\n\ndef expected_reduction(share, efficacy):\n    return failure_rate * share * efficacy   # absolute drop in failure rate\n\nprint(f\"freshness fix: {expected_reduction(0.38, 0.8):.1%} points\")   # 2.4%\nprint(f\"bigger model:  {expected_reduction(0.12, 0.5):.1%} points\")   # 0.5%\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: a ticket-triage agent's failed runs\nAn agent that reads a ticket, looks up the account and files it to a queue fails on about 9% of runs\n(illustrative). The team codes 150 random failed trajectories by first bad step.\n\n| First error (stage) | Count | Share | Typical step |\n|---|---:|---:|---|\n| Account ID invented instead of looked up (tool arguments) | 51 | 34% | 1-2 |\n| Tool error ignored, filed anyway (tool result handling) | 33 | 22% | 3 |\n| Wrong queue, correct context (model) | 27 | 18% | 4 |\n| Should have asked the user (policy) | 21 | 14% | 2 |\n| Step budget hit while retrying (control flow) | 18 | 12% | 6+ |\n\n- **Fixes by stage:** a validator that rejects account IDs not returned by a prior lookup attacks 34%;\n  returning structured errors and blocking `file_ticket` after a failed lookup attacks another 22%.\n- **The model is again a small target:** only 18% are wrong decisions with correct context.\n- **Uncertainty:** the top share has standard error √(0.34 × 0.66 / 150) ≈ 0.039, so roughly 26%-42%.","patterns":["**Annotation queue for failed runs.** When: any agent in production. How: route a random sample of runs with a failure signal (tool error, step budget hit, thumbs-down, human takeover) plus a slice of normal runs into a queue where reviewers tag first bad step and stage. Monitor: share per category and median step of first error.","**Weekly failure review.** When: any system in production. How: a fixed slot where an engineer and a domain expert code a fresh random sample of flagged traces plus a slice of unflagged traffic into the shared taxonomy. Monitor: category shares week over week and the size of \"other\".","**Promote categories to detectors.** When: a category is stable and recurring. How: write a rule, validator or LLM-judge check for it and run it on all traffic and in the regression eval. Monitor: the detector's rate, spot-checked against manual labels.","**Inter-coder agreement check.** When: the taxonomy is new or changed. How: two people code the same few dozen cases and compare; rewrite any definition they split on. Monitor: share of cases with agreement.","**Fix-confirmation sample.** When: after shipping a stage fix. How: draw the next random sample from a window that starts after the fix and recompute that category's share with its interval. Monitor: the share falling outside the previous interval.","**Stage tag in the trace schema.** When: designing tracing. How: record per-stage outputs (retrieval results, tool arguments, validation errors) so coders can name the originating stage quickly. Monitor: fraction of cases coded \"unknown stage\"."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agents fail in more places than chatbots: wrong tool chosen, malformed\n  arguments, a tool error ignored, a loop, a missed hand-off to a human. Reading a sample of trajectories\n  tells you which of these actually dominates, so the next week of work goes there.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Trace viewers or an annotation queue for labelling runs, a shared\n  taxonomy of failure categories, and the eval suite where top categories become regression cases.\n- **What you control.** Which signals mark a run as failed, how you sample, the taxonomy and severity\n  scale, and whether each trace records per-step outputs so the originating stage can be named.\n- **What you can skip.** Heavy statistics. Proportions with a standard error and a frequency × severity\n  score are enough to rank fixes.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Deciding between a model upgrade and stage fixes.** A coded sample shows what share of agent failures\n  a stronger model could even reach.\n- **Prioritising a tool-calling agent's backlog.** Invented arguments, ignored tool errors and loops are\n  counted and ranked rather than argued about.\n- **End-of-pilot reviews with a customer.** Their experts code failures with you, so severity reflects\n  their business, not your assumptions.\n- **Building the agent's regression eval.** Each top category becomes a detector or a set of eval cases\n  tracked on every change.","mini_project":"**Run a failure analysis on 40 agent trajectories (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** produce a ranked, stage-tagged list of what breaks a small tool-calling agent.\n- **Steps:** run a simple agent (2-3 tools) on 100-200 varied tasks, including hard and ambiguous ones,\n  and log every step; pick 40 failed runs at random (all of them if fewer fail) plus 10 successes; for\n  each, note the first bad step, its stage and a severity from 1 to 4; cluster into categories; compute\n  shares with 95% intervals and a severity-weighted score; implement the cheapest fix for the top\n  category and rerun.\n- **Done when:** you have a table of categories with shares and intervals, one fix shipped at its\n  originating stage, and a rerun showing whether that category's share fell."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"Failure signals: explicit, implicit, system, eval\"] --> R[\"Random sample plus some successes\"]\n    A[\"Random slice of all traffic\"] --> C\n    R --> C[\"Code first error and stage\"]\n    C --> T[\"Cluster into taxonomy\"]\n    T --> K[\"Count share with error bars\"]\n    K --> W[\"Weight by severity and reach\"]\n    W --> F[\"Fix top category at its stage\"]\n    F --> D[\"Detector or eval case\"]\n    D -->|\"next sample\"| R","caption":"The loop runs from biased-signal sampling to a ranked list; a fix is confirmed only when the next sample\nshows its category shrinking."},"interview":[{"q":"What is failure analysis and how does it differ from debugging?","a":"- **Definition:** a pass over a random sample of many failures that codes each by first error and stage,\n  then ranks categories by frequency, severity and reach.\n- **Why it differs:** debugging explains one failure; failure analysis decides which fix removes the most\n  harm, so effort stops following the loudest report."},{"q":"Your satisfaction score fell from 82% to 76% over two weeks. Walk me through what you do.","a":"- **Sample:** a few hundred random flagged conversations from the drop window, some successes, and a\n  random slice of unflagged traffic.\n- **Code:** read full traces, note the first error and its stage, cluster into categories, double-code a\n  subset to check definitions.\n- **Rank and act:** shares with error bars, weighted by severity; fix the top category at its originating\n  stage and confirm with the next sample."},{"q":"When would you not run a failure-analysis pass?","a":"- **During an active severe incident.** One harmful failure needs mitigation and debugging now, not a\n  sampling exercise.\n- **When volume is tiny.** With a handful of failures, read them all; sampling statistics add nothing.\n- **When the taxonomy is stable and automated**, the weekly manual pass can shrink to spot checks, though\n  periodic fresh reading still catches new categories."},{"q":"A category is 15% of 200 sampled failures. How confident are you in that number?","a":"- **Standard error** is √(0.15 × 0.85 / 200) ≈ 0.025, so the 95% interval is roughly 10%–20%.\n- **Good enough to rank** against a 38% category, not good enough to distinguish it from a 12% one.\n- **To compare close categories**, sample more or accept that severity should decide the order."},{"q":"Why code only the first error in a trace?","a":"- **Cascades inflate counts.** A wrong retrieval produces a wrong citation and a wrong answer; counting all\n  three makes downstream stages look guilty.\n- **Trade-off:** you can miss a second, independent defect, so record secondary errors in a separate field\n  when you suspect one."},{"q":"Your agent's task success rate dropped from 91% to 84%. How do you decide what to fix?","a":"- **Sample trajectories, not answers.** Pull a random set of failed runs plus some successes and read each\n  run step by step to find the first bad step and its stage.\n- **Tag agent-specific stages:** tool choice, tool arguments, tool result handling, retrieval or memory,\n  control flow and policy, plus the step number.\n- **Rank and fix at the stage:** for example a validator for invented IDs or structured tool errors, then\n  confirm with the next sample before considering a model swap."}],"tradeoffs":["**Manual reading vs expert hours.** Reading traces finds categories nobody anticipated but costs expert hours. It flips toward automated classifiers once the taxonomy is stable and volume makes reading every week impractical.","**Larger samples vs diminishing returns.** Larger samples narrow the confidence intervals, but ranking the top few modes rarely needs more than a few hundred cases. Spend extra effort on rare, high-severity categories instead.","**Fine-grained taxonomy vs sparse counts.** Fine categories give precise fixes but few cases each. Merge categories when most have only a handful of cases.","**First-error coding vs compounding issues.** Coding only the first error keeps counts honest but hides compounding issues. Record secondary errors separately when you suspect two independent defects."],"pitfalls":["**Analysing only the failures customers forwarded.** Symptom: a priority list that tracks your largest accounts rather than your most common defects. Fix: random sampling.","**Reading only final answers instead of full traces.** Symptom: almost everything is labelled \"model hallucinated\" and no stage-specific fix emerges. Fix: read the trace.","**Counting cascaded errors as separate failures.** Symptom: category totals exceed the number of cases, and fixes all point at the same upstream defect. Fix: code the first error.","**Building the automated classifier before a manual pass.** Symptom: a large \"other\" bucket and categories that never change week to week. Fix: open coding first.","**Ranking by frequency alone.** Symptom: the team polishes tone while a rare data-exposure or wrong-commitment failure keeps recurring. Fix: weight by severity.","**Sampling only flagged conversations.** Symptom: a reported failure rate that looks stable while customers, on manual review, turn out to be receiving confidently wrong answers nobody flagged. Fix: label a random slice of all traffic.","**Labelling the visible bad action instead of the first bad step.** Symptom: an agent taxonomy dominated by \"wrong action\" with no stage-specific fix. Fix: walk the trajectory back to the first step whose output was wrong, often a tool argument or ignored tool error."],"when":"- **Use when:** an aggregate quality metric moves, before choosing between expensive interventions (model\n  swap, fine-tuning, re-architecture), at the end of a pilot, and on a regular cadence for any system in\n  production.\n- **Avoid when:** you need incident response. A single severe failure needs debugging and mitigation now,\n  not a sampling exercise.\n- **Measure:** category shares over time; a fix is confirmed when its category's share falls in the next\n  sample.\n- **Interviews:** answering \"quality dropped, what do you do?\" with \"sample failures, categorise by stage,\n  rank by impact\" signals maturity.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Pulls the sample, codes traces, and converts the top failure categories into automated detectors and regression eval cases that run on every change.","architect":"Uses the stage attribution to decide where to invest, for example retrieval freshness infrastructure versus model upgrades, and ensures traces exist to make analysis possible.","fde":"Runs the analysis with the customer's subject-matter experts, whose judgement of what counts as a failure and how severe it is often differs from the vendor's assumptions."},"check":{"q":"Leadership wants to fine-tune a model because \"accuracy is 78%\". How do you decide whether\nfine-tuning is the right investment?","a":"I would sample a few hundred failing cases at random, read the full traces and code the first error\nwith its originating stage. Fine-tuning mainly addresses failures where the model had the right context and\nstill answered wrongly, or where learned behaviour is the issue (output format, tool-argument conventions,\nwhen to escalate). If most failures originate in retrieval, data freshness or infrastructure, fine-tuning\ncannot fix them, and even model-behaviour failures are often cheaper to fix with validators or prompt rules,\nso the money is better spent at those stages. I would show the category shares with confidence intervals, weight them by severity, and\nestimate the ceiling each intervention could reach before committing."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should failure coding anchor on the first thing that went wrong in a trace?","options":["The first error is always the most severe one","Later errors are often consequences of the first, so counting them separately inflates downstream categories","Models only make one error per conversation","It makes the analysis faster because the rest of the trace can be skipped"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A wrong retrieval often causes a wrong citation and then a wrong answer; attributing all three to the originating defect keeps counts honest and points the fix at the stage that actually caused the failure."},{"q":"Which failure sample is the least biased basis for prioritisation?","options":["All conversations escalated by the five largest customers","Every thumbs-down from the past day","A random sample from all failure signals over a fixed window, plus a few successes for comparison","The cases the support team flagged as most embarrassing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Forwarded or explicit-feedback samples over-represent loud accounts and engaged users; random sampling across several signal types estimates true proportions, and successes show whether a pattern is failure-specific."},{"q":"In a sample of 200 failures, 12% are model reasoning errors with correct context. What does that imply about switching to a stronger model?","options":["It will fix most failures because stronger models also retrieve better","It is pointless because 12% is within the margin of error","It directly targets roughly that share and cannot fix retrieval or infra failures, so other stages likely offer larger gains","It should be done first because model errors are the hardest to fix otherwise"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A stronger model improves generation, not stale indexes or provider timeouts; its main lever is the share of failures that originate in the model (plus some spillover into tool arguments and instruction following), so larger categories elsewhere usually deserve priority."}]},{"key":"delivery-ownership/incident-severity-and-escalation","sources":["Beyer, Jones, Petoff, Murphy (eds.), 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering","Beyer, Murphy, Rensin, Kawahara, Thorne (eds.), 2018 — The Site Reliability Workbook"],"why":"- **Without levels, every incident starts with a negotiation.** One engineer thinks a 5% error rate is a\n  shrug; another pages the VP.\n- **Both directions fail.** Small issues wake people at 3 a.m. and burn out on-call, while serious ones\n  sit in a ticket queue because whoever noticed did not feel entitled to raise the alarm. Customers get\n  updates erratically, and the contractual clock in an SLA runs while people debate.\n- **\"Use judgement\" fails under incident conditions:** stress, partial information, and someone senior\n  asking whether it is really that bad.\n- **Classifying by cause fails too** (\"it's just a config bug\"), because cause is unknown at the start and\n  the same root cause can be trivial or catastrophic depending on how many users it reaches.\n- **AI incidents are often silent.** A conventional outage is loud: errors spike, health checks fail. An\n  AI incident returns HTTP 200 with fluent, wrong, or unsafe content. If criteria only mention\n  availability and latency, a model giving incorrect medical dosage information, or leaking one tenant's\n  documents into another's answers, may not register as an incident at all.","how":"### Severity is defined by impact\nA typical scale has four or five levels. The exact criteria are organisation-specific; a common shape:\n\n| Level | Impact | Response |\n|---|---|---|\n| **SEV1 / critical** | Broad user-facing outage or degradation of a core function, data exposure or security breach, safety harm, or legal or regulatory exposure | All hands, leadership informed |\n| **SEV2 / major** | Significant degradation for a meaningful share of users or a key customer, with no workaround | Urgent, paged response |\n| **SEV3 / minor** | Limited impact or a workaround exists | Working hours, owning team |\n| **SEV4 / low** | Cosmetic or internal-only | Backlog |\n\n- **Write criteria as observable facts:** fraction of requests or tenants affected, whether data crossed a\n  tenant boundary, whether a contractual SLA is at risk.\n- **Why observable:** responders must classify within minutes, with incomplete information.\n\n### AI-specific criteria\nAdd impact criteria about content, not uptime:\n- **Unsafe or harmful outputs** reaching users.\n- **Cross-tenant or PII leakage**, including through prompt injection.\n- **An agent taking unauthorised or irreversible actions.**\n- **A measured quality metric** (groundedness, task success) falling past a defined threshold.\n- **A provider model change or outage** affecting all traffic.\n- **One case can be enough.** A single confirmed data-exposure case can justify the top severity even if\n  it touched one user, because the harm is not proportional to volume.\n\n### Agent action criteria\nAgents act, so classify by what the action did and whether it can be undone:\n\n| Agent behaviour | Typical severity | First mitigation |\n|---|---|---|\n| Irreversible or unauthorised action (payment, deletion, external email) | SEV1 | Disable that tool or switch the agent to read-only |\n| Reversible wrong actions at scale (mis-filed tickets, wrong tags) | SEV2 | Require human approval for that tool |\n| Agent loops or step budget hit, cost spiking, no user harm | SEV3 | Lower the step cap or route to a fallback |\n| Odd tool choice with correct outcome | SEV4 | Backlog, add an eval case |\n\n- **Count actions, not just requests.** One run can call a side-effecting tool many times, so the share of\n  degraded requests understates impact.\n- **Treat the table as a starting point.** Harm and reach still decide: one irreversible but trivially\n  small action may be SEV2, while reversible wrong actions that hit a key customer's records can be SEV1.\n\n### Declare early, adjust freely\n- **Declare at the higher plausible severity and downgrade as facts arrive**, *because* under-declaring\n  delays response while over-declaring costs only some attention.\n- **Severity is not a verdict on anyone.** It is a routing decision and should change as the picture\n  changes.\n- **The asymmetry is for humans, not alerts.** It applies to a human declaring a suspected incident, not\n  to automated alert thresholds. A page that fires on noise has a real cost, because people learn to\n  ignore it, so tune alerts for precision while declaration stays biased high.\n\n### Severity is not priority\n- **Severity measures impact; priority is work order.** Priority also weighs urgency (is the harm growing\n  or stable?) and available effort.\n- **Example:** a SEV3 with a workaround can still top the queue because the workaround expires tomorrow.\n- **Map customer priorities explicitly.** Contracts usually define their own ticket priorities (P1 to P4\n  or similar) with response targets. Without an explicit mapping to internal severities, a customer's P1\n  lands as an internal SEV3 and the SLA clock runs out while both sides think the other is on it.\n- **Bug vs incident:** a defect becomes an *incident* when it needs a coordinated response now, not when\n  it is merely a bug in the backlog.\n\n### Severity drives the response\nEach level maps to:\n1. who is paged;\n2. a target time to acknowledge and to mitigate;\n3. whether an incident commander is assigned;\n4. the update cadence for stakeholders and customers;\n5. whether a postmortem is mandatory.\n\n- **Split the roles.** The commander coordinates and decides; others investigate, communicate, or scribe.\n  This stops the person debugging from also fielding questions from executives.\n\n### Two kinds of escalation\n| | Functional escalation | Hierarchical escalation |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Brings in** | Expertise or ownership: the retrieval team, the platform on-call, the model provider's support with request IDs and timestamps | Authority: a decision to take a feature offline, notify customers or regulators, spend money, or override a customer's change freeze |\n| **Trigger** | Current responders cannot make progress, or the fault is in someone else's component | The needed decision is above the responder's remit |\n\n- **Make both time-based.** If a SEV2 is not mitigated within the policy's window, it escalates\n  automatically, so no one has to judge whether asking for help is admitting failure.","example":"### Worked timeline: severity changes as facts arrive\nAn illustrative policy (the targets are examples, not a standard) and a timeline for an enterprise RAG\nassistant.\n\n**Policy excerpt:** SEV2 = a key customer or more than 5% of requests degraded with no workaround; page\non-call, acknowledge within 15 minutes, updates every 30 minutes, escalate to engineering manager if not\nmitigated in 60 minutes. Any confirmed cross-tenant data exposure = SEV1.\n\n| Time | What happened | Severity |\n|---|---|---|\n| **09:10** | Alert: 4% of responses fail JSON schema validation (later traced to a provider model alias moving to a newer snapshot). Failures fall back to a generic answer. Workaround exists, small share. | SEV3 |\n| **09:25** | Scoping: all failures are from one enterprise tenant whose prompts use a tool schema the new snapshot handles differently; for them, 60% of requests fail. That tenant is a named account with an SLA. Incident commander assigned; account team informed. | SEV2 |\n| **09:40** | Functional escalation: ticket to the provider with ten request IDs. Mitigation: pin that tenant to the previous model snapshot. | SEV2 |\n| **09:55** | Failure rate back to baseline. Customer update sent. Stays open until the schema is made compatible with the new snapshot. | SEV3 |\n| **10:30** | During review, an engineer notices one fallback answer contained a document title from a different tenant's cache. Security on-call paged, leadership and legal informed, cache disabled. | SEV1 |\n\nThe severity changed three times (SEV3, SEV2, SEV3, SEV1), each time because new facts about impact\narrived, not because the cause changed.\n\n### The policy as code (runnable)\nEncoding the excerpt makes the rule testable and keeps cause out of the inputs. Pure Python.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Impact:\n    share_of_requests_degraded: float   # 0.0-1.0, across all traffic\n    key_customer_degraded: bool         # a named account with an SLA\n    workaround_exists: bool\n    cross_tenant_exposure: bool = False\n    unsafe_output_reached_user: bool = False\n    cosmetic_only: bool = False\n\ndef classify(i: Impact) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Illustrative policy: impact facts in, severity out. Cause is not an input.\"\"\"\n    if i.cross_tenant_exposure or i.unsafe_output_reached_user:\n        return \"SEV1\"                               # harm is not proportional to volume\n    if (i.key_customer_degraded or i.share_of_requests_degraded > 0.05) and not i.workaround_exists:\n        return \"SEV2\"\n    if i.cosmetic_only:\n        return \"SEV4\"\n    return \"SEV3\"\n\ndef needs_escalation(sev: str, minutes_unmitigated: int) -> bool:\n    return sev == \"SEV2\" and minutes_unmitigated >= 60   # time-based, from the policy excerpt\n\nprint(classify(Impact(0.04, False, True)))                     # SEV3 at 09:10\nprint(classify(Impact(0.04, True, False)))                     # SEV2 at 09:25\nprint(classify(Impact(0.0, False, True)))                      # SEV3 at 09:55\nprint(classify(Impact(0.0, False, True, cross_tenant_exposure=True)))  # SEV1 at 10:30\nprint(needs_escalation(\"SEV2\", 45), needs_escalation(\"SEV2\", 60))      # False True\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: duplicate refunds from a support agent\nA support agent can call `issue_refund`. A retry change makes it re-issue a refund when the tool times\nout but the payment actually went through (timeline illustrative).\n\n| Time | What happened | Severity |\n|---|---|---|\n| **14:05** | Alert: `issue_refund` calls per hour up about 25% while ticket volume is flat. No errors, no complaints. | SEV3 |\n| **14:20** | Trace sample: 9 of 40 runs called `issue_refund` twice for the same order. Money left the company and cannot be pulled back automatically. | SEV1 |\n| **14:23** | Mitigation: `issue_refund` disabled by flag, agent tells users a human will follow up. Finance and leadership informed. | SEV1 |\n| **15:10** | Duplicates listed from traces and handed to finance; idempotency key added to the tool before re-enabling. | SEV2 until reconciled |\n\nThe step that moved it to SEV1 was not volume but the fact that the action was irreversible.","patterns":["**Per-tool kill switches for agents.** When: any agent with side-effecting tools. How: a flag per tool that removes it from the agent's tool list or forces human approval, plus a read-only mode, all switchable without a deploy. Monitor: side-effecting calls per run and time from declaration to tool disabled.","**Severity matrix in the runbook header.** When: before the first incident. How: a one-screen table of observable criteria per level, with paging target, acknowledge and mitigate targets, update cadence and postmortem requirement. Monitor: how often severity was disputed or changed during incidents.","**Customer priority mapping.** When: any enterprise contract. How: a written table mapping each contractual priority (P1 to P4) to an internal severity and on-call rotation, stored with the SLA. Monitor: customer P1s acknowledged inside the contractual target.","**Content-level alerts.** When: any AI feature in production. How: page on online quality or safety signals (groundedness score dropping past a threshold, cross-tenant document IDs in a response, guardrail block rate spiking), not only on errors and latency. Monitor: alert precision, so pages stay trusted.","**Timer-driven escalation.** When: SEV1 and SEV2. How: the paging or incident tool escalates automatically if the incident is not acknowledged or mitigated within the policy window, and the commander can defer with a stated reason. Monitor: time to acknowledge and time to mitigate by severity.","**Per-tenant kill switches.** When: multi-tenant AI products. How: flags that pin one tenant to a previous model snapshot, disable a cache or retrieval path, or switch a feature off without a deploy. Monitor: time from declaration to first mitigation."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent can do damage while every dashboard is green: it returns HTTP\n  200 while issuing a duplicate refund, emailing the wrong customer or leaking another tenant's document.\n  Severity criteria that only mention uptime will not page anyone for that.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Alerts on tool-action rates, guardrail blocks and quality scores;\n  per-tool and per-tenant kill switches; the paging tool's escalation timers; and the provider request\n  IDs you attach when escalating to your model vendor.\n- **What you control.** Which agent behaviours count as SEV1 (irreversible or unauthorised actions, data\n  exposure), which mitigations exist without a deploy (disable a tool, read-only mode, pin a snapshot),\n  and who can flip them.\n- **What you can skip.** Designing an organisation-wide incident programme from scratch; adopt the\n  company's scale and add agent-specific criteria and switches to it.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Agents with payment or account tools.** Irreversible actions get their own SEV1 criterion and a\n  per-tool kill switch before launch.\n- **Multi-tenant RAG agents.** A single cross-tenant document in an answer is a top-severity incident even\n  with normal error rates.\n- **Silent provider model updates.** A schema or tool-call regression for one key tenant escalates to SEV2\n  and is mitigated by pinning that tenant's snapshot.\n- **Enterprise agent contracts.** Customer P1 to P4 priorities are mapped to internal severities so the\n  SLA clock and the on-call rotation agree.","mini_project":"**Write and test a severity policy for a tool-calling agent (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** turn agent-specific impact facts into a tested classifier plus the switches it relies on.\n- **Steps:** list your agent's tools and mark each reversible or irreversible; extend the `Impact`\n  dataclass from the example with fields such as irreversible action taken and tenant boundary crossed;\n  write `classify` and `needs_escalation`; add a flag that removes a named tool from the agent's tool list\n  at runtime; write tests for five scenarios, including duplicate refunds and a loop with no user harm.\n- **Done when:** all five scenarios classify as your policy says, the flag disables a tool without a\n  restart, and the one-screen severity matrix is written down next to the code."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    D[\"Signal or report\"] --> Q1{\"Data exposure, safety harm or legal risk?\"}\n    Q1 -->|\"yes\"| S1[\"SEV1: all hands, leadership\"]\n    Q1 -->|\"no\"| Q2{\"Key customer or broad share degraded, no workaround?\"}\n    Q2 -->|\"yes\"| S2[\"SEV2: page, commander\"]\n    Q2 -->|\"no\"| Q3{\"Cosmetic or internal only?\"}\n    Q3 -->|\"no\"| S3[\"SEV3: owning team, work hours\"]\n    Q3 -->|\"yes\"| S4[\"SEV4: backlog\"]\n    S2 -->|\"not mitigated in window\"| E[\"Auto-escalate to manager\"]\n    S3 -.->|\"new impact facts\"| Q1","caption":"Impact facts, not cause, pick the level; new facts can move it either way at any time."},"interview":[{"q":"Why define severity by impact rather than by cause?","a":"- **Cause is unknown at the start.** Responders must classify in minutes, and impact (share of requests,\n  tenants affected, data crossing a boundary) is observable early.\n- **The same cause scales differently.** A config bug can be trivial for one internal user or catastrophic\n  for every tenant, so cause cannot set the response."},{"q":"An enterprise customer files a P1, but your on-call classifies it SEV3. What went wrong and how do you fix it?","a":"- **The mapping is missing.** Contract priorities and internal severities are separate scales, so without\n  an explicit table the SLA clock runs while each side assumes the other is acting.\n- **Fix:** write the P1-to-P4 mapping into the escalation policy, route customer P1s to the paged rotation,\n  and review disputed cases in the next postmortem."},{"q":"When would you not page someone for a quality drop?","a":"- **When the online metric is untrustworthy.** A noisy groundedness score that fires on normal variation\n  teaches people to ignore pages, which is worse than a slower page.\n- **When a workaround holds and harm is stable**, it is a SEV3 for working hours, not a 3 a.m. page.\n- **Do page** when the drop crosses a validated threshold or involves unsafe content or data exposure."},{"q":"How would you classify a hallucination that reached one customer?","a":"- **By harm, not count.** A wrong cosmetic detail is SEV3 or SEV4; a wrong medical dosage or a wrong\n  contractual commitment can justify SEV1 or SEV2 even for one user.\n- **Then check reach.** Search traces for the same pattern, because one report often means many unnoticed\n  cases.\n- **If an agent acted on it**, classify the action: a hallucinated order ID used in a cancellation is an\n  irreversible-action incident, not a wording issue."},{"q":"Your agent just sent 30 emails to the wrong customers and errors are at zero. What do you do in the first 15 minutes?","a":"- **Declare high.** An external, irreversible action with possible data exposure is SEV1 provisionally;\n  assign a commander and inform security and the account owner.\n- **Mitigate without a deploy:** disable the email tool by flag or switch the agent to read-only, then\n  snapshot the traces for the affected runs.\n- **Escalate hierarchically** for customer and possibly regulatory notification, and functionally to the\n  team that owns the recipient lookup the agent called."},{"q":"What numbers would you track to know your severity policy works?","a":"- **Time to declare, acknowledge and mitigate**, broken down by severity.\n- **Severity changes per incident** and disputed classifications, which point to unclear criteria.\n- **Share of incidents first reported by customers** rather than monitoring, which reveals detection gaps,\n  especially for silent AI failures."}],"tradeoffs":["**Low thresholds vs on-call fatigue.** Low thresholds catch real incidents early but page people for noise. They flip toward higher thresholds when fatigue causes alerts to be ignored, which is worse than a slower page.","**Detailed criteria vs speed.** Detailed criteria reduce argument but take time to apply under pressure. Keep the top levels simple and observable, and put nuance in the lower levels.","**Automatic escalation vs interruptions.** Time-based escalation removes social friction but can pull managers into incidents that are nearly resolved. Allow the commander to acknowledge and defer with a stated reason.","**Quality incidents vs false alarms.** Treating quality degradations as incidents protects users but requires a trustworthy online quality metric. Without one, quality incidents generate false alarms and are quietly ignored."],"pitfalls":["**Defining severity only by uptime and error rate.** Symptom: a harmful or wrong-answer episode that ran for days because every dashboard stayed green. Fix: add content-level criteria.","**Classifying by suspected cause.** Symptom: \"it's only a config change\" incidents that turn out to affect every tenant. Fix: classify by impact.","**Reluctance to declare or escalate for fear of blame.** Symptom: incidents first heard about from customers or account managers rather than from monitoring or on-call. Fix: declare high, blameless culture, time-based escalation.","**One person as commander, debugger and communicator.** Symptom: customer updates that stop for an hour exactly when the incident is worst. Fix: split roles.","**Escalating to a model provider without request IDs, timestamps and a minimal reproduction.** Symptom: a support ticket that sits unanswered while you wait. Fix: attach the evidence.","**Severity inflation.** Everything urgent-sounding is declared SEV1 and never downgraded. Symptom: SEV1 pages that no longer mobilise anyone and a postmortem backlog nobody can clear. Fix: downgrade as facts arrive.","**Shipping side-effecting agent tools with no off switch.** Symptom: the only mitigation for a misbehaving tool is a full rollback or taking the whole agent down. Fix: per-tool flags and a read-only mode, tested before launch."],"when":"- **Use when:** any team runs a production service. Write the scale and escalation policy before the\n  first incident, agreed with support and account management and, for enterprise deals, aligned with the\n  SLA's priority definitions.\n- **Revisit:** after each postmortem that revealed a classification dispute. For AI systems, add\n  content-level criteria and the online signals that detect them.\n- **Measure:** time to declare, time to acknowledge and time to mitigate by severity, and how often\n  severity changed during incidents.\n- **Interviews:** expect \"how would you classify a hallucination that reached one customer?\" and answer by\n  impact and harm, not by error rate.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes alerts that map to severity criteria, carries the pager, and knows how to pin a model snapshot or disable a feature as the first mitigation.","architect":"Defines the severity scale with AI-specific impact criteria and ensures the system has kill switches and per-tenant controls that make fast mitigation possible.","fde":"Aligns the customer's own severity scale and contacts with the vendor's, so an incident in the customer's environment escalates to the right people on both sides within minutes."},"check":{"q":"One user reports that the assistant quoted a paragraph from a document they should not have access\nto. Error rates and latency are normal. What severity do you assign and what happens next?","a":"Top severity, provisionally. Impact, not volume, drives classification: a single confirmed access-\ncontrol breach is a data exposure with legal and contractual consequences, and it may affect many users\nwho simply did not notice. I would page security and the owning team, assign a commander, preserve the\ntrace, and mitigate fast, for example by disabling the retrieval path or the cache involved. Hierarchical\nescalation covers customer and possibly regulatory notification. If investigation shows the user did\nhave access, we downgrade; declaring high and downgrading is cheaper than the reverse."},"quiz":[{"q":"What should primarily determine an incident's severity level?","options":["How difficult the root cause is expected to be to fix","Which team's component is suspected to be at fault","The observed impact on users, data, safety and contractual obligations","How long the system has been in production"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cause and ownership are usually unknown when an incident starts, and the same cause can be trivial or severe; impact is observable early and determines how urgent and broad the response must be."},{"q":"What distinguishes hierarchical escalation from functional escalation?","options":["Hierarchical escalation is automatic, functional escalation is manual","Hierarchical brings in authority to make decisions; functional brings in expertise or the owning team","Hierarchical escalation is only used for security incidents","Functional escalation always goes to an external vendor"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Functional escalation adds people who can diagnose or fix the component; hierarchical escalation adds people who can approve decisions such as taking a feature offline or notifying customers."},{"q":"Why do incident policies tell responders to declare at the higher plausible severity?","options":["Higher severity incidents are exempt from postmortems","It guarantees the SLA clock stops running","Customers expect every incident to be reported as critical","Under-declaring delays the response while over-declaring only costs attention, and severity can be lowered later"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The costs are asymmetric: a late response to a real SEV1 compounds harm, whereas downgrading after facts arrive is cheap, so the policy biases toward early, high declaration."}]},{"key":"delivery-ownership/root-cause-analysis-and-postmortems","sources":["Beyer, Jones, Petoff, Murphy (eds.), 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering","Richard I. Cook, 1998 — How Complex Systems Fail","John Allspaw, 2012 — Blameless PostMortems and a Just Culture"],"why":"- **Mitigation ends the incident, not the next one.** Roll back, close the ticket and move on, and the\n  same class of failure returns in a different shape, because whatever let it through (a missing test, a\n  silent alert, a deploy path without review) is still there.\n- **Stopping at the proximate cause fails.** \"A bad prompt change was deployed\" names the trigger and\n  suggests \"be more careful\", which is not an action anyone can execute.\n- **Blame fails worse.** \"The engineer skipped the eval\" feels like accountability but produces the\n  opposite: people learn to hide mistakes and sanitise timelines, so the organisation loses the very\n  information it needs.\n- **Blame also misreads how failures happen.** In complex systems, incidents typically need several latent\n  conditions to line up, and the human action at the end was usually reasonable given what that person\n  could see at the time.\n- **AI makes stopping early tempting.** \"The model is non-deterministic\" seems like an explanation; it is\n  not. A well-built system expects wrong model outputs and has grounding checks, validation, guardrails\n  and human review sized to the risk. The question is why none of those caught this one.","how":"### 1. Build the timeline first\n- **Assemble a factual, timestamped timeline** from logs, alerts, chat and deploy records.\n- **Record:** when the trigger happened, when impact began, when it was detected and by whom, each\n  decision and its rationale, when it was mitigated and resolved.\n- **Why first:** memory is reconstructive. The timeline anchors discussion in evidence and exposes gaps,\n  such as a 40-minute stretch between impact and detection.\n\n### 2. Ask \"why\" along several chains\n| Technique | What it does | Limit or use |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Five whys** | Repeatedly asks why the previous answer happened | Gets past the trigger, but produces one linear chain and stops wherever the asker's knowledge or patience runs out |\n| **Ishikawa (fishbone)** | Groups candidate causes by category: code, data, model, process, people, environment | Avoids tunnel vision |\n| **Fault tree** | Works backwards from the failure through AND/OR gates | Shows that several conditions were jointly necessary |\n\nRun the whys along several axes:\n1. why did the defect exist;\n2. why did it reach production;\n3. why was it not detected sooner;\n4. why did mitigation take as long as it did.\n\n### 3. Prefer contributing factors over \"the root cause\"\n- **List every condition** without which the incident would not have happened or would have been smaller.\n- **Why:** this framing is more accurate for complex systems and produces better actions, because each\n  factor is a separate place to add a defence.\n- **The \"Swiss cheese\" picture:** each layer of defence has holes, and an incident is the rare moment they\n  line up, so shrinking the holes in any one layer helps.\n- **Counterfactual test:** had this condition been absent, would the incident have been prevented or\n  smaller? If not, it is background, not a factor.\n- **Evidence test:** is the causal link shown by logs, traces or a reproduction, or only plausible? A\n  plausible story that nobody checked is how postmortems confidently fix the wrong thing.\n\n### The defence layers in an agent\nWhen an agent incident happens, walk each layer and ask why it did not catch the bad step:\n\n| Layer | Question for the postmortem |\n|---|---|\n| Tool design | Could the tool do this at all? Was it scoped to the task (read-only, one tenant, capped amount)? |\n| Argument validation | Did anything check the arguments against a prior lookup or a schema? |\n| Approval gate | Should this action have needed a human confirmation? |\n| Control flow | Was there a step cap, a retry limit, an idempotency key? |\n| Evals | Did the regression suite contain a trajectory like this one? |\n| Online detection | Which metric or alert should have fired, and why did it not? |\n\nEach \"no\" is a contributing factor and a candidate action.\n\n### When to stop asking why\n- **Stop at a changeable, ownable condition:** one a team can change with a system control and own as an\n  action.\n- **One step too far** usually lands on something true but useless (\"people make mistakes\", \"the deadline\n  was tight\").\n- **One step short** leaves a trigger (\"a bad config was pushed\").\n- **Organisational endings get escalated.** If the chain ends in a factor such as no budget for eval\n  coverage, record it and escalate it as a structural finding rather than pretending a ticket can close it.\n\n### 4. Stay blameless, not accountability-free\n- **Describe actions by the information and pressures people had:** \"the eval suite passed because it\n  contained no cases for policy documents\", not \"Sam didn't test properly.\"\n- **Goal:** a safe environment for honest accounts. Accountability lives in owning the action items.\n\n### 5. Write the postmortem\nA standard structure:\n1. summary;\n2. impact (users, duration, data, money, SLA);\n3. timeline;\n4. detection (how and how fast);\n5. response;\n6. contributing factors;\n7. what went well;\n8. where we got lucky, which captures near-misses that would otherwise be forgotten;\n9. action items.\n\n### 6. Action items that actually close\n- **Each action has** a single owner, a due date, a tracking ticket and a category.\n- **Categories:** **prevent** (stop the defect existing), **detect** (catch it faster), **mitigate** (limit\n  blast radius or speed recovery).\n- **Change the system, not memory.** Prefer a gate, a test, an alert or a kill switch over actions that\n  rely on people remembering.\n- **Review open actions on a fixed cadence.** An action with no owner or date is a wish.\n\n### 7. Share and learn\n- **Publish postmortems internally** and look across them periodically.\n- **Recurring factors** (no eval coverage for new document types, provider changes going unnoticed) are\n  where structural investment pays off.","example":"### Worked postmortem: the 12-week leave policy\n**Incident:** for six hours an HR assistant told employees the parental-leave policy was 12 weeks; the\ncurrent policy is 16. **Proximate trigger:** a re-indexing job ingested an archived policy folder.\n\n**Five whys along the *defect* chain:**\n1. The wrong answer was generated because the old PDF ranked top.\n2. It ranked top because both versions were indexed with no effective-date metadata.\n3. There was no metadata because the ingestion connector was configured by folder, and the archive sat\n   inside the indexed folder.\n4. Nobody noticed because the connector config was not code-reviewed.\n\n**Along the *detection* chain:**\n- The issue was found by an employee complaint, not monitoring.\n- The groundedness check verified that answers matched retrieved text (they did) rather than that the\n  source was current.\n- There were no eval cases asserting current policy values.\n\n**Contributing factors:** archive inside an indexed path; no effective-date filter; unreviewed connector\nconfig; eval suite with no freshness cases; detection reliant on users.\n\n| Category | Action | Owner | Due |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Prevent | Index only documents with an effective date and filter superseded versions | retrieval team | 2 weeks |\n| Prevent | Connector configuration in version control with review | platform | 3 weeks |\n| Detect | Run 30 \"current value\" questions from HR after every re-index and nightly, since a nightly-only check could be slower than the six hours it took a user to complain | AI engineer and HR SME | 2 weeks |\n| Mitigate | Per-collection kill switch to exclude a source within minutes | platform | 4 weeks |\n\nNote what is absent: \"the model hallucinated\" and \"the admin should have checked the folder.\"\n\n### Worked agent postmortem: the cancelled orders\n**Incident (illustrative):** over 50 minutes an order-support agent cancelled 23 orders whose customers\nhad only asked about delivery dates. **Proximate trigger:** a tool-description edit widened `cancel_order`\nto read as the general \"order changes\" tool.\n\n| Layer | What the trace showed | Contributing factor |\n|---|---|---|\n| Tool design | One tool's description covered both order edits and cancellation | Over-broad tool |\n| Approval gate | Cancellation needed no confirmation from the user | Missing gate for an irreversible action |\n| Evals | No trajectory case where the user asks a question and no action should be taken | Eval gap |\n| Change process | Tool descriptions were edited outside code review | Unreviewed prompt surface |\n| Detection | Found by a customer call; cancellations per hour had no alert | User-detected |\n\n| Category | Action | Owner | Due |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Prevent | Narrow `cancel_order` to cancellation only and require explicit user confirmation | agent team | 2 weeks |\n| Prevent | Tool descriptions in version control with review and an eval run | platform | 3 weeks |\n| Detect | 40 \"question only, no action\" trajectories in the regression suite, plus an alert on cancellations per hour | AI engineer | 2 weeks |\n| Mitigate | Per-tool kill switch for `cancel_order` | platform | 1 week |\n\nAbsent again: \"the model misunderstood\" and \"the engineer should have tested the description.\"\n\n### Linting action items and spotting recurring factors (runnable)\nA small check you can run in CI over postmortem action items exported from your tracker. Pure Python.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import Counter\nfrom datetime import date\n\nCATEGORIES = {\"prevent\", \"detect\", \"mitigate\"}\nactions = [  # from the leave-policy postmortem, plus one weak item\n    {\"id\": \"PM-12-1\", \"category\": \"prevent\", \"owner\": \"retrieval team\", \"due\": date(2026, 3, 2), \"ticket\": \"RET-88\", \"done\": True},\n    {\"id\": \"PM-12-2\", \"category\": \"prevent\", \"owner\": \"platform\", \"due\": date(2026, 3, 9), \"ticket\": \"PLAT-41\", \"done\": False},\n    {\"id\": \"PM-12-3\", \"category\": \"detect\", \"owner\": \"AI engineer\", \"due\": date(2026, 3, 2), \"ticket\": \"AI-17\", \"done\": True},\n    {\"id\": \"PM-12-4\", \"category\": \"mitigate\", \"owner\": \"platform\", \"due\": date(2026, 3, 16), \"ticket\": \"PLAT-42\", \"done\": False},\n    {\"id\": \"PM-12-5\", \"category\": \"prevent\", \"owner\": None, \"due\": None, \"ticket\": None, \"done\": False},  # \"be more careful\"\n]\n\ndef lint(a):\n    problems = [f for f in (\"owner\", \"due\", \"ticket\") if not a.get(f)]\n    return problems + ([] if a.get(\"category\") in CATEGORIES else [\"category\"])\n\ntoday = date(2026, 3, 12)\nfor a in actions:\n    if p := lint(a):\n        print(a[\"id\"], \"is a wish, missing:\", \", \".join(p))   # PM-12-5\n    elif not a[\"done\"] and a[\"due\"] < today:\n        print(a[\"id\"], \"overdue\")                             # PM-12-2\ndone = sum(a[\"done\"] for a in actions)\nprint(f\"completion {done}/{len(actions)} = {done / len(actions):.0%}\")   # 2/5 = 40%\n\n# Factors that recur across postmortems point at structural work\nfactors = [[\"no freshness evals\", \"unreviewed config\"], [\"no freshness evals\", \"user-detected\"],\n           [\"provider change unnoticed\", \"user-detected\"]]\nprint(Counter(f for pm in factors for f in pm).most_common(2))\n# [('no freshness evals', 2), ('user-detected', 2)]\n```","patterns":["**Postmortem template with mandatory sections.** When: every incident above the policy's severity threshold. How: a shared document template with the nine sections above, including \"where we got lucky\", started by the incident scribe during the incident. Monitor: days from resolution to review.","**Fixed-cadence action review.** When: always. How: a recurring meeting or report that lists open postmortem actions by owner and due date, with overdue items escalated to the owning manager. Monitor: action-item completion rate and median age of open items.","**Tag contributing factors with a shared vocabulary.** When: you have more than a handful of postmortems. How: label each factor from an agreed list (freshness, eval gap, config review, provider change, detection by user) so they can be counted across incidents. Monitor: share of new incidents repeating an earlier factor.","**Every detection gap becomes an eval or alert.** When: an AI incident was found by a user. How: add the failing inputs as regression eval cases and an online check (for example a scheduled \"current value\" question set). Monitor: time from impact start to detection on the next incident.","**Every agent incident becomes a trajectory eval.** When: an agent took a wrong action or loop. How: save the run's inputs and recorded tool results as a regression case that asserts the correct tool sequence (or no action), and run it on every prompt, tool-description or model change. Monitor: incidents whose trajectory pattern was already in the suite (should trend to zero).","**Internal and external versions.** When: customers were affected. How: keep the internal postmortem fully candid and derive a customer version with impact, timeline summary and corrective actions. Monitor: customer follow-up questions the external version failed to answer."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agents will make wrong calls; the postmortem question is why the\n  harness around the model (tool permissions, validators, approval gates, step caps, evals) let a wrong\n  call become harm. That is where the fixes live.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Run traces and deploy logs for the timeline, the agent's eval suite\n  for new regression cases, tool wrappers for validators and idempotency, and flags for kill switches.\n- **What you control.** Which defences sit between a model decision and a side effect, how fast a bad\n  pattern is detected, and whether every postmortem action lands as a test, gate or alert with an owner.\n- **What you can skip.** Formal reliability methods beyond five whys, a fishbone and a simple fault tree;\n  those three are usually enough for agent incidents, with heavier methods kept for safety-critical\n  domains.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **An agent took an irreversible action it should not have.** The postmortem walks tool design,\n  validation, approval gates and evals rather than stopping at the model's choice.\n- **A RAG agent served stale facts.** Freshness and detection chains produce index filters and scheduled\n  \"current value\" checks.\n- **Recurring agent incidents across teams.** Tagged contributing factors (eval gap, unreviewed tool\n  description, provider change) show where platform investment pays.\n- **Customer-facing agent outages.** An external version of the postmortem explains impact and fixes to\n  the customer while the internal one stays candid.","mini_project":"**Write a postmortem for a staged agent incident (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** practise turning one agent failure into prevent, detect and mitigate actions that are code.\n- **Steps:** give a small agent a side-effecting fake tool and a description that invites misuse; run 20\n  tasks and capture the traces where it misfires; write the timeline and walk the six defence layers; ship\n  at least one action per category (a validator or confirmation gate, a trajectory eval case, a kill\n  switch); run the action-item linter from the example over your list.\n- **Done when:** the postmortem has every section of the template, the linter reports no wishes, and the\n  same 20 tasks rerun with zero misfires and the new eval case passing."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    I[\"Incident mitigated\"] --> T[\"Timeline from logs and records\"]\n    T --> W1[\"Why did the defect exist\"]\n    T --> W2[\"Why did it reach production\"]\n    T --> W3[\"Why was detection slow\"]\n    T --> W4[\"Why was mitigation slow\"]\n    W1 --> F[\"Contributing factors\"]\n    W2 --> F\n    W3 --> F\n    W4 --> F\n    F -->|\"counterfactual and evidence tests\"| A[\"Actions: prevent, detect, mitigate\"]\n    A -->|\"owner, date, ticket\"| R[\"Tracked and reviewed\"]\n    R -.->|\"recurring factors\"| S[\"Structural investment\"]","caption":"Several \"why\" chains feed one list of tested factors; only owned, dated actions leave the review."},"interview":[{"q":"What does \"blameless\" mean in a postmortem, and why does it matter?","a":"- **Definition:** actions are described by the information and pressures people had, not as personal\n  failings, while accountability lives in owned action items.\n- **Why:** blame makes people hide mistakes and sanitise timelines, so the organisation loses the evidence\n  it needs to fix the system."},{"q":"An agent incident was detected 40 minutes after impact began. How do you analyse that gap?","a":"- **Treat detection as its own chain.** Ask why no alert fired, which signal would have caught it (for an\n  agent: side-effecting calls per hour, steps per run, tool error rate), and who noticed first.\n- **Check the evidence:** did monitoring look at the right property (source freshness, not just\n  groundedness)?\n- **Output a detect action:** an alert or eval with an owner, date and ticket, measured by time to detect\n  on the next incident."},{"q":"When would you not write a full postmortem?","a":"- **Low-severity incidents with no near-miss potential**: use a short template so senior time goes to\n  the incidents that teach the most.\n- **Always write one** for data exposure or safety events regardless of scale, and for near-misses that\n  would have been severe."},{"q":"How do you know your postmortem process is working?","a":"- **Action-item completion rate**: a process whose actions stay open changes nothing.\n- **Recurrence of contributing factors**: if new incidents keep sharing factors with earlier ones, the\n  structural fixes are not landing.\n- **Time to detect and mitigate** trending down for similar incident classes."},{"q":"Five whys versus a fault tree: when do you use which?","a":"- **Five whys** is fast for getting past the trigger, but follows one chain and stops where knowledge ends.\n- **A fault tree** works back through AND/OR gates and is the better tool when you must show several\n  conditions were jointly necessary.\n- **In practice** run whys along several axes and use a fishbone or fault tree when the chains branch."},{"q":"An agent issued a wrong refund. The draft postmortem blames the model. How do you run the analysis?","a":"- **Start from the trace:** which step produced the amount, what the tool returned, and what the model saw.\n- **Walk the defence layers:** why the amount was not fetched by a tool, why no validator compared it with\n  the order record, why no approval gate applied, why no eval covered refunds.\n- **Actions per layer** (prevent, detect, mitigate), each with an owner and date; a prompt change may be\n  one of them, never the only one."}],"tradeoffs":["**Depth vs senior time.** Deep analysis of every incident yields learning but consumes senior time. Reserve full postmortems for higher severities and near-misses with high potential impact, and use a short template for the rest.","**Contributing factors vs priority.** The framing is more accurate than a single root cause, but can produce a long list with no priority. Rank actions by how many future failure paths each one closes.","**Wide publication vs sensitivity.** Publishing widely spreads lessons, but candid detail can be sensitive for customers. Share an external version with impact and corrective actions, and keep the internal one fully candid.","**Systemic fixes vs speed.** Gates, tests and automation are durable but slower to ship than process reminders. Flip toward a temporary process control only as a bridge, with a dated plan for the systemic fix."],"pitfalls":["**Stopping at the trigger** (\"bad deploy\", \"model hallucinated\"). Symptom: action items like \"be more careful\" and the same class of incident recurring within months. Fix: ask why along every chain.","**Blame, explicit or implied.** Symptom: vague timelines, defensive meetings and fewer incidents reported even as customer complaints rise. Fix: describe information and pressures, not people.","**Action items without owners, dates or tickets.** Symptom: a postmortem archive whose actions are mostly still open when the next similar incident happens. Fix: lint and review them.","**Only \"prevent\" actions.** Symptom: a different trigger produces the same long, user-detected outage because detection and mitigation never improved. Fix: require detect and mitigate actions.","**Writing the postmortem weeks later.** Symptom: a timeline reconstructed from memory with gaps exactly where the important decisions were made. Fix: start the timeline during the incident.","**Hindsight bias**, judging decisions by an outcome nobody could see at the time. Symptom: \"should have\" and \"failed to\" language in the timeline, and no explanation of why the action looked reasonable to the person who took it. Fix: record what each person knew when they acted.","**Treating the agent's decision as the root cause.** Symptom: actions like \"improve the system prompt\" and the same kind of wrong action recurring with different wording. Fix: walk the defence layers and add a gate, validator or eval at each one that failed."],"when":"- **Use when:** an incident is above a severity threshold defined in your incident policy, for any data\n  exposure or safety event regardless of scale, and for near-misses that would have been severe.\n- **Timing:** start the timeline while the incident is fresh and hold the review within days.\n- **Measure:** action-item completion rate and how often incidents share contributing factors with\n  earlier ones; both tell you whether postmortems are changing anything.\n- **Interviews:** a strong answer separates trigger, contributing factors and detection gaps, and proposes\n  system changes rather than personal vigilance.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the timeline from traces and deploy logs, and turns detection gaps into concrete eval cases, alerts and regression tests with owners.","architect":"Looks across postmortems for recurring structural factors and funds the platform changes, such as release gates or source freshness controls, that close whole classes of failure.","fde":"Writes the customer-facing version, joins joint reviews with the customer's teams, and makes sure factors on the customer's side become actions they own."},"check":{"q":"A postmortem draft says: \"Root cause: the LLM hallucinated a refund amount. Action: improve the\nprompt.\" What is wrong with it, and how would you rewrite the analysis?","a":"Hallucination is an expected behaviour of the component, so it is a symptom, not a cause; the\nquestion is why the system let an unverified amount reach a customer. I would ask why the amount was\ngenerated rather than looked up from the order system, why no validator compared it to the order record,\nwhy evals did not cover refund amounts, and how the issue was detected. Actions would then be: fetch\namounts via a tool, validate before sending, add eval cases, and alert on mismatches, each with an owner\nand date. \"Improve the prompt\" may stay, but only as one item among them."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the main limitation of the five-whys technique in complex systems?","options":["It requires specialised software to run","It follows one linear chain and stops where the asker's knowledge ends, missing parallel contributing factors","It can only be applied to hardware failures","It always assigns the cause to an individual"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Incidents usually need several conditions at once; a single chain of whys finds one of them. Running it along prevention, detection and mitigation axes, or using a fishbone or fault tree, surfaces the rest."},{"q":"Why are blameless postmortems more effective than assigning individual fault?","options":["They remove the need for anyone to own follow-up actions","They shorten the postmortem meeting","People give honest, detailed accounts when they are not punished, which reveals the system conditions behind the error","Regulators require that individuals never be named"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Blame drives people to hide or sanitise what happened, destroying the information needed to fix the system; blamelessness keeps accounts honest while accountability lives in owned action items."},{"q":"Which action item best addresses a detection gap in an AI incident found by a user complaint?","options":["Remind the team to review outputs more carefully during releases","Switch to a more capable model","Add a disclaimer that answers may be inaccurate","Add an automated eval or online check for the failure type, with an owner and due date"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A detection action should catch the failure automatically before users do; reminders and disclaimers do not detect anything, and a model swap is a prevention guess rather than a detection control."}]},{"key":"delivery-ownership/operational-ownership-and-support-boundaries","sources":[],"why":"- **A deployed AI system is a stack with many owners:** the model provider's API, your platform and\n  serving layer, the application's prompts, retrieval and tools, the customer's identity provider,\n  network and data sources, and often a customer-built front end.\n- **Every owner's first instinct is \"not mine\".** When an answer is wrong or a request fails, the cause\n  can be in any layer, and each owner tries to show it is not theirs. The customer just sees \"your AI is\n  broken.\"\n- **Implicit ownership does not scale.** Whoever built it, or whoever is most helpful, fixes it. In\n  forward-deployed work the integration engineer keeps getting called months after go-live, for systems\n  they no longer control. That makes them a single point of failure and hides the true support cost.\n- **Rigid \"not our problem\" boundaries fail the other way.** With no path across them, a ticket about\n  stale answers is closed because the stale documents live in the customer's SharePoint.\n- **What explicit boundaries buy:** every component has someone who will act, every party knows what it\n  must do, and cross-boundary issues have a defined route instead of a debate.","how":"### Owners have pager, access and authority\nA team owns a component only if it carries all three together:\n1. **Pager:** it is paged when the component fails.\n2. **Access:** it can diagnose and change it.\n3. **Authority (and budget):** it can prioritise fixes.\n\n- **Missing one breaks it.** Ownership without access produces helpless on-call; access without ownership\n  produces drive-by changes no one is accountable for.\n- **A service catalog records** each component's owner, on-call rotation, runbook, dependencies and\n  support hours.\n\n### Draw the boundary by layer\n- **For each layer, state who operates it, who changes it, and who owns its inputs.**\n- **The usual artefact is a shared-responsibility table**, sometimes as RACI (Responsible, Accountable,\n  Consulted, Informed), *because* \"who must act\" and \"who must be told\" are different questions, and\n  conflating them causes either paralysis or surprise.\n- **Exactly one Accountable per row** is the rule that makes RACI useful. Several Responsible parties can\n  share the work, but if two parties are Accountable each can wait for the other, and a row with none is\n  unowned by construction.\n\n### Supported configurations and coverage\n- **Scope: what you support.** A supported-configuration list names the versions, models, connectors and\n  deployment shapes you will diagnose. A customer who swaps in their own model or edits the system prompt\n  has moved outside it; the agreement should say whether support then becomes best-effort.\n- **Coverage: when you support it.** A 24×7 SEV1 commitment needs a staffed rotation (or follow-the-sun\n  across time zones). Business-hours support must say what happens to a SEV1 at 02:00 on Saturday.\n- **Mismatched hours are a classic gap**, because each side's on-call assumes the other's is awake.\n\n### Define the crossing points\nMost real incidents span a boundary, so the interface matters most:\n- **Evidence** the reporting party must provide: request IDs, timestamps, tenant.\n- **Channel** to use.\n- **Response targets** by severity.\n- **Who leads** joint incidents.\n- **\"Out of scope\" means routing**, \"we route it to the right owner with evidence\", not \"we close the\n  ticket.\"\n\n### Tiered support\n| Tier | Does | Needs |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Tier 1** | Takes the report, gathers evidence, resolves known issues from runbooks | Runbooks, evidence checklist |\n| **Tier 2** | Diagnoses using logs and tooling | Dashboards, trace access |\n| **Tier 3** | The engineering team that can change code, prompts or models | Clean escalations |\n\n- **Each tier needs documentation to resolve what it can**, *because* every issue that reaches\n  engineering unnecessarily is expensive and slows real fixes.\n- **Measure** the share of tickets resolved at each tier and the share escalated with complete evidence.\n  A tier 3 that keeps asking \"which request ID?\" is doing tier 1's job.\n\n### Plan the handover\n- **Phases:** build, launch, a period of elevated attention (hypercare) and steady state.\n- **Ownership changes at defined points with exit criteria:** a stable error and quality baseline,\n  runbooks validated by the receiving team, and alerts routed to their on-call.\n- **Without this, the build team never actually lets go.**\n\n### AI-specific boundaries\nMake explicit what is new in AI systems; these are the questions most likely to be unowned by default:\n- **Answer quality:** owned by the prompt and eval owner, not the platform.\n- **Source data freshness and access permissions:** often the customer.\n- **Model snapshot changes:** who decides when a provider snapshot changes.\n- **Harmful-output reports:** who responds.\n\n### Agent-specific boundaries\nAgents add layers that default to nobody:\n\n| Layer | Usual owner | Why it gets orphaned |\n|---|---|---|\n| Agent behaviour (tool choice, task success) | Agent team, via prompts and evals | Each tool team sees only its own calls |\n| Each tool's implementation and API | The team that owns the wrapped system | The agent team wrote the wrapper, so is assumed to own the system |\n| Tool permissions and scopes | Security or the system owner | Granted once at build time, never reviewed |\n| Actions taken on a user's behalf | Agent team for the decision, system owner for the effect | Both assume the other will reverse it, so name one Accountable for reversal |\n\n- **Tool contracts:** for each tool, record its owner, its error format and its on-call, because a tool\n  that changes its response shape breaks the agent with no error on either side.","example":"### Worked boundary table: RAG assistant in a bank's private cloud\nA vendor deploys a RAG assistant into a bank's private cloud. The boundary table agreed at handover:\n\n| Layer | Operates | Changes | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Model endpoint (cloud provider) | Provider | Vendor chooses snapshot | Vendor escalates to provider |\n| Application, prompts, retrieval, evals | Vendor | Vendor | Quality incidents are the vendor's |\n| Kubernetes cluster, network, SSO | Bank platform team | Bank | Vendor has read-only logs |\n| Source document repositories | Bank content owners | Bank | Freshness and permissions are the bank's |\n| Chat front end in the intranet | Bank web team | Bank | Vendor supplies API only |\n\n**Week 9 ticket:** \"answers cite last year's expense policy.\"\n1. Tier 1 at the bank collects three request IDs.\n2. Vendor tier 3 traces them: retrieval returned the 2025 document because the 2026 one was uploaded to a\n   folder not included in the connector scope.\n3. **Cause** is on the bank's side (document placement); **the detection gap** is on the vendor's (no\n   freshness eval).\n4. **Resolution:** the bank's content owner moves the file and adopts a publishing checklist; the vendor\n   adds a freshness eval and an alert when a source's newest document is older than a set threshold.\n5. **Outcome:** resolved in a day because both sides knew who did what, and neither closed it as \"not\n   ours\".\n\n### Validating a RACI table (runnable)\nEncode the boundary table and check the one rule that matters: exactly one Accountable, at least one\nResponsible. Pure Python.\n\n```python\nRACI = {  # layer -> {party: role letters}\n    \"Model endpoint\":             {\"Provider\": \"R\", \"Vendor\": \"A\"},\n    \"App, prompts, retrieval\":    {\"Vendor\": \"RA\", \"Bank platform\": \"I\"},\n    \"Cluster, network, SSO\":      {\"Bank platform\": \"RA\", \"Vendor\": \"C\"},\n    \"Source documents\":           {\"Bank content\": \"RA\", \"Vendor\": \"I\"},\n    \"Answer quality\":             {\"Vendor\": \"A\", \"Bank content\": \"A\"},   # \"shared\": a trap\n    \"Harmful-output reports\":     {\"Vendor\": \"R\", \"Bank platform\": \"C\"},  # nobody accountable\n}\n\ndef check(raci):\n    problems = []\n    for layer, parties in raci.items():\n        accountable = [p for p, roles in parties.items() if \"A\" in roles]\n        responsible = [p for p, roles in parties.items() if \"R\" in roles]\n        if len(accountable) != 1:\n            problems.append(f\"{layer}: {len(accountable)} Accountable {accountable}\")\n        if not responsible:\n            problems.append(f\"{layer}: nobody Responsible\")\n    return problems\n\nfor p in check(RACI):\n    print(p)\n# Answer quality: 2 Accountable ['Vendor', 'Bank content']\n# Answer quality: nobody Responsible\n# Harmful-output reports: 0 Accountable []\n```\n\n### Worked ticket: an IT-helpdesk agent and three tool owners\nAn internal agent resets passwords, checks laptop orders and files tickets, each through a different\nteam's API (scenario illustrative).\n\n| Time | Event | Owner who acts |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Mon 09:00** | Tier 1 sees order-status answers saying \"no orders found\" for 30% of runs | Agent on-call (owns task success) |\n| **Mon 09:40** | Traces show `get_orders` returns an empty list with HTTP 200 since the procurement API's Friday release | Agent team routes to procurement on-call with run IDs |\n| **Mon 11:00** | Procurement confirms a renamed field; rolls forward a fix | Procurement (owns the tool's API) |\n| **Mon 12:00** | Agent team adds a contract test on the tool's response and an alert on empty-result rate | Agent team (owns its detection gap) |\n\nNeither team closed it as \"not ours\": procurement owned the cause, the agent team owned detection.","patterns":["**Catalog entry per agent tool.** When: an agent calls tools owned by other teams. How: register each tool (or tool server) with its owning team, rotation, response contract and permissions, and route tool error alerts there while task-success alerts go to the agent team. Monitor: tool calls whose owner entry is missing or stale.","**Service catalog as source of truth.** When: more than a few components or teams. How: every component has an entry with owning team, on-call rotation, runbook link, dependencies and support hours, and alert routing reads from it. Monitor: entries whose owner or rotation no longer exists.","**Boundary table in deployment readiness.** When: any multi-party deployment. How: agree the layer table with the customer before go-live, mirror it in the SLA, runbooks and support tooling, and rehearse it in a tabletop exercise. Monitor: tickets bounced between parties.","**Evidence-required escalation form.** When: tickets cross from customer or tier 1 to engineering. How: the escalation template requires request IDs, timestamps, tenant and severity before it can be submitted. Monitor: share of tier 3 tickets escalated with complete evidence.","**Hypercare with dated exit criteria.** When: every launch. How: a time-boxed window where builders stay first line, ending when the baseline is stable, runbooks are validated by the receiving team and alerts route to their on-call. Monitor: pages to builders after the exit date (should be near zero).","**Read-only, audited vendor access.** When: the vendor operates inside a customer environment. How: grant read access to logs and traces through the customer's audited access path rather than broad admin rights. Monitor: time to diagnose and access-review findings."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent is glue across other people's systems: a model provider, your\n  orchestration and prompts, tools wrapping other teams' APIs, and the customer's data. When a run fails,\n  someone must own each of those, and someone must own the agent's overall task success.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The service catalog entry for the agent and each tool or tool\n  server, the on-call rotation that alerts route to, the escalation template, and the boundary table in\n  the deployment agreement.\n- **What you control.** Naming one Accountable owner per layer (including \"agent behaviour\" and \"each\n  tool\"), what evidence a ticket must carry (run ID, step, tool), and the hypercare exit criteria.\n- **What you can skip.** Formal ITIL process detail; a boundary table, a catalog and a tested escalation\n  route cover what an agent team needs.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Internal agents over many team APIs.** Tool owners carry the pager for their APIs; the agent team owns\n  task success, prompts and evals.\n- **Agents deployed into a customer environment.** A boundary table splits the model endpoint, the agent,\n  the cluster and the customer's data sources before go-live.\n- **FDE-built agents after launch.** Hypercare with dated exit criteria moves support off the person who\n  built it.\n- **Agents acting on users' behalf.** Someone is named to reverse a wrong action, not just to fix the\n  agent that took it.","mini_project":"**Write and validate the ownership map for a small agent (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** make every layer of an agent you have built owned by exactly one Accountable party.\n- **Steps:** list the agent's layers (model endpoint, orchestration and prompts, each tool, data sources,\n  permissions, actions on behalf of users); fill a RACI table and run the validator from the example over\n  it; write a one-page escalation template requiring run ID, step and tool name; add a contract test for\n  one tool's response shape.\n- **Done when:** the validator reports no problems, every tool has a named owner and rotation, and the\n  contract test fails when you rename a field in a fake tool response."},"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant U as User\n    participant T1 as Customer tier 1\n    participant V3 as Vendor tier 3\n    participant CO as Customer content owner\n    U->>T1: Answers cite last year's policy\n    T1->>T1: Collect request IDs and timestamps\n    T1->>V3: Escalate with evidence\n    V3->>V3: Trace retrieval for each request\n    V3->>CO: Route cause: file outside connector scope\n    CO->>CO: Move file, adopt publishing checklist\n    V3->>V3: Add freshness eval and alert\n    V3->>T1: Resolved, both actions owned","caption":"A ticket crosses the boundary with evidence; each side fixes its own factor, and nobody closes it as\n\"not ours\"."},"interview":[{"q":"What makes a team the real owner of a production component?","a":"- **Three things together:** it is paged when the component fails, it has access to diagnose and change\n  it, and it has authority and budget to prioritise fixes.\n- **Why all three:** ownership without access gives helpless on-call; access without ownership gives\n  drive-by changes nobody answers for."},{"q":"A customer says your RAG agent's answers are stale and the documents live in their system. How do you handle it?","a":"- **Route, do not close.** Collect request IDs, trace retrieval, and send the cause to the customer's\n  content owner with evidence.\n- **Own your side too.** Ask why your monitoring did not detect it, and add a freshness eval or alert as\n  your action."},{"q":"When would you not use \"you build it, you run it\"?","a":"- **When many small services share infrastructure**, a dedicated platform or operations team avoids every\n  product team carrying a thin, tiring rotation.\n- **When the builders are FDEs**, because keeping them first line stops them moving to new deployments;\n  limit it to a dated hypercare window."},{"q":"Your SLA promises 24×7 SEV1 response, but the customer's platform team works business hours. What breaks?","a":"- **Joint incidents stall at night.** If the fault is in their cluster or SSO, your on-call is awake but\n  cannot change it, and the SLA clock still runs.\n- **Fix:** agree a customer-side out-of-hours contact for SEV1, or scope the commitment to layers you\n  operate, and test the route in a tabletop exercise."},{"q":"How would you measure whether your support boundaries work?","a":"- **Tickets bounced between parties** and time to route an issue to its owner.\n- **Share of tickets reaching tier 3** that tier 1 or 2 could have resolved, and share escalated with\n  complete evidence.\n- **Pages to builders after the hypercare exit date**, which should fall to near zero."},{"q":"Your agent calls tools owned by four other teams. How do you set up ownership before launch?","a":"- **One row per layer:** agent behaviour and task success (your team), each tool's API (its owning team),\n  permissions (security or the system owner), and actions on a user's behalf.\n- **Tool contracts and routing:** each tool has a catalog entry, a response contract test and an on-call;\n  tool errors page the tool owner, task-success drops page you.\n- **Crossing points:** tickets carry run ID, step and tool name, so routing takes minutes."}],"tradeoffs":["**\"You build it, you run it\" vs on-call load.** It aligns incentives and shortens feedback, but loads product teams with on-call. It flips toward a dedicated operations or platform team when many small services share infrastructure.","**Narrow boundaries vs customer frustration.** Narrow support boundaries keep costs predictable, but frustrate customers when problems straddle them. Widen them, or sell an extended tier, for strategic accounts where retention matters more than margin.","**FDEs as first line vs FDE capacity.** Customers get expert help, but FDEs cannot move to new deployments. It only works for a time-boxed hypercare window with a dated handover.","**Broad vendor access vs security burden.** Broad access inside a customer environment speeds diagnosis, but raises security and compliance burden. Read-only, audited access to logs and traces is usually the workable middle."],"pitfalls":["**Ownership assigned to a team without access or authority.** Symptom: on-call that can only open tickets for someone else, and mean time to mitigate measured in days. Fix: grant pager, access and authority together.","**Orphaned components after a reorganisation or project end.** Symptom: alerts routed to a channel nobody reads and a service catalog entry naming someone who has left. Fix: re-assign owners as part of every reorganisation.","**No owner for answer quality.** Symptom: a slow decline in user satisfaction with every team pointing at another layer and no one running evals. Fix: name the prompt and eval owner.","**Handover without exit criteria.** Symptom: the original builders still being paged months after go-live, often unofficially through direct messages. Fix: dated, verified exit criteria.","**Boundaries written only in the contract.** Symptom: support staff on both sides who have never seen the table and argue scope during an incident. Fix: put it in runbooks and tooling.","**Two parties marked Accountable for the same layer**, often \"shared\" data quality. Symptom: a known problem that stays open for weeks while each side waits for the other to schedule the fix. Fix: one Accountable per row.","**Assuming the agent team owns every tool it wraps.** Symptom: the agent on-call is paged for another team's API outage and can only open tickets. Fix: tool owners carry the pager for their tool; the agent team owns task success and the wrapper."],"when":"- **Use when:** before go-live of any production system; revisit at each phase change, reorganisation or\n  new integration.\n- **Multi-party AI deployments:** agree the boundary table with the customer as part of deployment\n  readiness, mirror it in the SLA, runbooks and support tooling, and test it with a tabletop exercise.\n- **Measure:** tickets bounced between parties, time to route an issue to its owner, and the share of\n  tickets reaching engineering that tier 1 or 2 could have resolved.\n- **Interviews:** expect \"after launch, who supports this?\" and answer with owners, crossing points and a\n  handover plan.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the runbooks, dashboards and evidence-gathering tools that let other tiers resolve issues without escalating to the engineers who wrote the code.","architect":"Draws component boundaries so each has one owner, and designs the access model and interfaces that let ownership cross organisations safely.","fde":"Negotiates the boundary table with the customer, runs hypercare, and executes the handover so the deployment does not depend on them personally after it ends."},"check":{"q":"Six months after launch, the FDE who built a customer's deployment still receives most of its\nsupport requests by direct message. What is wrong, and what would you change?","a":"Ownership was never transferred: the deployment has an implicit owner with no on-call rotation,\nno shared runbooks and no defined route for the customer's issues. That makes one person a single point\nof failure and hides the support cost. I would write the boundary table with the customer, register the\ncomponents in the service catalog with an owning team and rotation, route requests to a support queue\nwith tiering, make sure runbooks and dashboards let tier 2 diagnose common issues, and set handover exit\ncriteria the receiving team signs off. The FDE then becomes a tier 3 escalation, not the front door."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which combination makes a team a genuine operational owner of a component?","options":["It wrote the original code and is listed in the README","It is paged for failures, has access to diagnose and change it, and has authority to prioritise fixes","It has admin credentials to the production environment","It attends the weekly status meeting with the customer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Paging without access, or access without accountability, both fail; ownership requires being alerted, being able to act, and being empowered to choose the work."},{"q":"A user reports stale answers; the stale document lives in the customer's repository, outside your support scope. What is the correct response?","options":["Close the ticket as out of scope and inform the user","Silently fix the repository yourself using any access you have","Route it to the customer's content owner with evidence, and check whether your side should have detected it","Retrain the embedding model on the latest documents"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A boundary defines who acts, not whether the issue matters; out-of-scope issues are routed with evidence, and detection gaps on your side remain your action items."},{"q":"What is the main purpose of exit criteria for a hypercare period?","options":["To decide when the customer can stop paying for support","To guarantee no incidents happen after handover","To let the build team skip writing runbooks","To make the transfer of ownership a deliberate, verified step rather than something that never happens"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Without criteria such as a stable baseline, validated runbooks and alerts routed to the new owner, builders stay informally on the hook indefinitely and the receiving team is never ready."}]},{"key":"delivery-ownership/slas","sources":["Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering","Beyer et al., 2018 — The Site Reliability Workbook"],"why":"- **The buyer needs a written promise.** An enterprise is about to route a business process through your\n  agent. Procurement, legal and their operations team need to know what happens when it is down at\n  2 a.m., how fast someone answers, and what they are owed if it keeps happening.\n- **Unwritten expectations get set anyway.** Without an SLA they come from the best week of the pilot,\n  what a salesperson said on a call, or the customer's experience with a hyperscaler. Every outage then\n  becomes an argument about whether it \"counted\".\n- **The naive numbers fail predictably.**\n  - Copying a cloud provider's headline number ignores that your service sits on several dependencies,\n    each failing independently, so your honest ceiling is lower than any one of theirs.\n  - Promising what the pilot achieved ignores that it ran at a tenth of production traffic with the\n    builders watching it.\n  - Promising \"24/7 support\" with no rotation behind it turns the first night incident into a breach.\n  - Promising answer quality as if it were uptime creates a metric neither side measures the same way.\n- **A signed SLA changes what an incident is.** Downtime is no longer only an engineering problem: it\n  accrues credits, triggers reporting obligations and, past a threshold, termination rights. The delivery\n  owner has to understand that commercial clock as well as the technical one.\n- **Scope of this lesson.** SLI, SLO and error-budget mechanics live in reliability/slas-slos; this\n  lesson is about writing and operating the promise.","how":"### Anatomy of a service SLA\n1. **Service definition.** Which endpoints, features and environments are covered. Production only;\n   beta features and sandboxes are explicitly out, *because* you need somewhere to ship experiments.\n2. **Metric definition.** The SLI formula in one sentence a query can implement:\n   - share of valid requests returning a non-error response within the latency bound, or\n   - minutes in which a synthetic probe failed.\n   - **Measurement point:** your load balancer, not the customer's laptop, *because* both sides must\n     compute the same number from data both can inspect.\n   - **Partial outages:** need an explicit rule, usually a \"bad minute\" whose error rate exceeds a\n     threshold. Without it, a month of 3 percent errors is either invisible (probe minutes rarely fail)\n     or a total breach, depending on who is reading.\n   - **Valid requests:** requests over the rate limit, malformed requests and client cancellations are\n     normally excluded from the denominator.\n3. **Window and target.** Almost always a calendar month, *because* credits are paid against monthly\n   invoices. The allowance is `allowed_downtime = (1 − target) × minutes_in_window`.\n4. **Exclusions.** Scheduled maintenance with notice, customer-caused load beyond contracted limits,\n   force majeure, and features marked preview. Whether an upstream model provider's outage is excluded\n   is the most contested line in AI contracts; customers usually refuse, reasoning that your vendor\n   choice is your risk.\n5. **Remedies.** Tiered service credits as a percentage of the monthly fee, often stated as the sole\n   remedy, plus a termination right after repeated misses. Credits are usually claimed by the customer\n   within a stated period, not paid automatically.\n\n### The allowance table is a staffing statement\nFor a 30-day month (43,200 minutes):\n\n| Target | Allowed downtime per month | What it implies |\n|---|---|---|\n| 99 percent | 432 minutes | humans can notice and fix |\n| 99.5 percent | 216 minutes | a staffed rotation with fast paging |\n| 99.9 percent | 43.2 minutes | automated detection and rollback |\n| 99.95 percent | 21.6 minutes | automation plus tested failover |\n| 99.99 percent | about 4.3 minutes | redundancy across every dependency |\n\n- **Why 99.9 needs automation:** 43 minutes a month is less than one slow\n  page-acknowledge-diagnose-rollback cycle, so it cannot rely on a human noticing.\n\n### Support SLAs\n- **Promise response time.** The time until a qualified human acknowledges and starts work, per severity\n  level (see incident-severity-and-escalation).\n- **Treat resolution time as a target** or a workaround commitment, *because* you control when someone\n  picks up but not how long an unknown root cause takes to find.\n- **Coverage hours matter as much as minutes.** A 30-minute Sev1 response around the clock implies a\n  staffed on-call rotation, not one engineer with a phone.\n\n### Deriving what you can commit\n1. **List the serial dependencies** on the request path with their own SLAs.\n2. **Multiply them, then account for your own failures:** `ceiling = A_own × ∏ SLA_dependency`.\n3. **Order the numbers:** `SLA < internal SLO < ceiling`. Set the internal SLO below the ceiling and the\n   SLA below the SLO, *because* the gap is the room in which you detect and fix problems before they\n   become contract events.\n\n- **The ceiling is contractually backed, not a forecast.** It assumes failures are independent and each\n  dependency runs exactly at its SLA floor. Real availability may be better or, for some providers,\n  worse, so use measured history where you have it.\n- **Remember the asymmetry.** When your provider misses its SLA you receive credits on your provider\n  bill, while you owe credits on a customer contract that is usually far larger.\n\n### Operating the promise\nSigning is the start. The owner runs the promise all month:\n1. **Month-to-date budget.** Minutes consumed against the allowance, visible to the on-call team,\n   *because* a 20-minute incident on day 3 and on day 28 of a month that already burned 200 minutes are\n   different decisions.\n2. **Error-budget policy.** When most of the budget is gone, slow risky changes (prompt edits, model\n   swaps, migrations) until the window resets.\n3. **SLA-impact field on every incident.** Covered minutes, excluded or not, and why, filled in during\n   the postmortem rather than reconstructed at month end, *because* exclusion arguments are won with\n   contemporaneous evidence.\n4. **Month-end attainment report.** Built from the same query the contract describes; handle credit\n   claims against it.\n\nTwo commercial facts shape this:\n- **Credits are small.** They are commonly capped and stated as the sole remedy, so their value to the\n  customer is small next to the business they lost.\n- **Repeated misses are what customers escalate over,** which is why termination-on-repeated-breach\n  clauses are the ones that matter.\n\n### What AI changes\n- **Quality** rarely belongs in the SLA as a percentage. Put it in acceptance criteria, recurring eval\n  reports, and a commitment to remediate regressions within a set time.\n- **Model changes** need a notice clause: how far ahead you announce a model or prompt change that can\n  alter outputs, and whether the customer can pin a version.\n- **Latency** depends on output length, so define it as time to first token or per-request latency for\n  requests under a token cap; an unqualified p95 promise is broken by one long-answer workload.\n- **Throughput**: the SLA applies within contracted rate limits, *because* a 10x burst would otherwise\n  turn a provider quota into your breach.\n\n### What agents add\n- **Promise on the run, not the call.** Define a valid request as one agent run (user task in, answer or\n  explicit handoff out), *because* that is what the customer experiences. A run that retries a tool five\n  times and then succeeds is good; one that hits the step cap without an answer is bad.\n- **Calls per run multiply the ceiling.** If every run makes several model calls and tool calls in\n  series, each with its own failure rate, the run's availability is the product, not the model's number.\n  Retries and fallbacks raise it back; measure the run-level number, not the per-call one.\n- **Latency needs a bounded loop.** Run latency is roughly steps × (model time + tool time), so a p95\n  promise needs a step cap, a token cap per call and a wall-clock budget per run, with a graceful\n  \"partial answer plus handoff\" when the budget runs out.\n- **Agent actions need their own terms.** If the agent writes to customer systems, say what happens on a\n  failed or duplicated action (idempotent tools, reconciliation), *because* an uptime number says nothing\n  about a wrong write.","example":"### SLA calculator: allowance, attainment, credits and ceiling\nPure Python; the numbers match the worked month below.\n\n```python\nMINUTES = 30 * 24 * 60  # 43,200 in a 30-day month\nTIERS = [(0.95, 0.50), (0.99, 0.25), (0.995, 0.10)]  # (below this, credit share of fee)\n\ndef allowance(target, minutes=MINUTES):\n    return (1 - target) * minutes\n\ndef attainment(bad_minutes, minutes=MINUTES):\n    return 1 - bad_minutes / minutes\n\ndef credit(avail, fee):\n    for floor, pct in TIERS:          # most severe tier first\n        if avail < floor:\n            return pct * fee\n    return 0.0\n\ndef ceiling(own, *deps):\n    out = own\n    for d in deps:\n        out *= d\n    return out\n\nfor t in (0.99, 0.995, 0.999, 0.9995, 0.9999):\n    print(f\"{t:.2%} -> {allowance(t):.1f} min/month\")   # 432.0, 216.0, 43.2, 21.6, 4.3\n\nincidents = {\"provider outage\": 140, \"bad deploy\": 95}\nfor exclude_provider in (False, True):\n    bad = sum(m for name, m in incidents.items()\n              if not (exclude_provider and name.startswith(\"provider\")))\n    a = attainment(bad)\n    print(f\"exclude_provider={exclude_provider}: {bad} min, {a:.3%}, credit ${credit(a, 40_000):,.0f}\")\n# exclude_provider=False: 235 min, 99.456%, credit $4,000\n# exclude_provider=True: 95 min, 99.780%, credit $0\n\nprint(f\"ceiling = {ceiling(0.999, 0.999, 0.9995):.4%}\")   # 99.7502%\n```\n\n### Worked month: two incidents and a breach\nA customer pays $40,000 per month (illustrative). The SLA promises 99.5 percent monthly availability,\nmeasured as minutes in which a synthetic probe against the production API fails twice in a row.\n\n| Credit tier | Credit |\n|---|---|\n| below 99.5 percent | 10 percent of the fee |\n| below 99.0 percent | 25 percent |\n| below 95 percent | 50 percent |\n\nThe month has 30 days, so 43,200 minutes and an allowance of 216 minutes.\n\n| Day | Event | Budget left |\n|---|---|---|\n| 9 | Model provider regional outage, 140 minutes; fallback never configured for that region | 76 minutes for 21 days |\n| 9 | The error-budget policy should have frozen risky changes now; it did not | 76 |\n| 22 | Bad deploy breaks the API for 95 minutes | none: 235 minutes total |\n\n- **Attainment:** 1 − 235/43,200 ≈ 99.456 percent, a breach in the first tier: a $4,000 credit if the\n  customer files a claim.\n- **With a provider exclusion:** downtime would be 95 minutes, 99.78 percent, no breach, which is\n  exactly why that clause is fought over.\n- **The ceiling behind the number:** provider SLA 99.9 percent, vector database 99.95 percent, your own\n  service historically about 99.9 percent. The serial product is 0.999 × 0.9995 × 0.999 ≈ 0.9975, or\n  about 99.75 percent if each runs at those levels and fails independently.\n- **Margin:** a 99.5 percent SLA leaves about a quarter of a percent. A 99.9 percent SLA, which sales had\n  floated, would be breached in an ordinary month unless a tested cross-region or cross-provider\n  fallback existed.\n- **The asymmetry:** your provider bill is $6,000, so even a 10 percent provider credit returns $600\n  against the $4,000 you owe.\n\n### Worked scenario: run-level availability of a support agent\nA support agent's typical run makes 4 model calls, 2 tool calls to the customer's ticketing API and 1\nvector-store query, all in series. Per-call success rates are illustrative, taken from a month of logs.\n\n| Path | Per-call success | Calls per run | Run success |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Model provider | 99.9 percent | 4 | 0.999⁴ ≈ 99.60 percent |\n| Ticketing tool | 99.5 percent | 2 | 0.995² ≈ 99.00 percent |\n| Vector store | 99.95 percent | 1 | 99.95 percent |\n| Whole run, no retries | | 7 | ≈ 98.56 percent |\n| Whole run, one retry per failed call | | | ≈ 99.99 percent (if failures are independent) |\n\n- **The lesson:** the per-call numbers look like \"three nines\", but without retries one run in about 70\n  fails, far below a 99.5 percent run-level SLA.\n- **Retries are not free:** each retry adds latency, so the step cap and wall-clock budget must leave room\n  for them, and correlated outages (a provider region down) defeat retries entirely; only a fallback model\n  or region helps there.\n\n```python\ndef run_success(per_call, calls, retries=0):\n    fail = (1 - per_call) ** (retries + 1)      # assumes independent failures\n    return (1 - fail) ** calls\n\nparts = [(0.999, 4), (0.995, 2), (0.9995, 1)]   # model, ticketing tool, vector store\nfor r in (0, 1):\n    total = 1.0\n    for p, n in parts:\n        total *= run_success(p, n, r)\n    print(f\"retries={r}: run success {total:.2%}\")  # 98.56%, 99.99%\n```","patterns":["**Contract-exact SLI query** — when signing any SLA, write the attainment query first and attach its definition (measurement point, bad-minute rule, valid-request filter) to the contract. Run the same query for the internal dashboard and the monthly report; monitor any gap between it and the customer's own monitoring.","**Burn-rate alerts on the internal SLO** — when the SLA allowance is under an hour, alert on how fast the stricter SLO budget is burning (a fast window for paging, a slow one for tickets) rather than on the SLA threshold. Monitor pages per month and time from first bad minute to acknowledgement.","**Error-budget change freeze** — when month-to-date budget consumption passes an agreed share, block prompt edits, model swaps and migrations through a deploy-pipeline gate until the window resets. Monitor how often the freeze triggers and how many changes it deferred.","**SLA-impact fields in the incident template** — on every incident, require covered minutes, the exclusion claimed and the evidence link before the postmortem closes. Monitor the share of incidents closed with the field empty.","**Tested fallback before raising the number** — when sales asks for a higher tier, first run a scheduled failover drill to a secondary provider or region and record the switch time. Monitor fallback success rate and the drill's measured switch time against the allowance.","**Run-level SLI from agent traces** — emit one record per agent run (run id, outcome, steps, wall-clock time, which dependency failed) and compute attainment over runs, not model calls. Monitor run success rate, step-cap hits and the dependency most often named in failed runs.","**Bounded agent loops** — set a step cap, a per-call token cap and a wall-clock budget per run, and return a partial answer with a human handoff when the budget runs out. Monitor run latency p95 against the contracted bound and the share of runs ending in handoff."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** One agent run makes many model and tool calls in series, so its\n  availability is the product of every dependency on the path, and one slow step blows a latency\n  promise. The SLA you can sign is set by your loop design, not by your model provider's headline.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Model provider quotas and SLAs, tool and vector-store\n  dependencies, per-run step and token caps, fallback model routing, and the attainment query over your\n  agent's request logs.\n- **What you control.** The unit you promise on (a run, not a model call), step and token caps that bound\n  run latency, which tools are on the critical path, fallbacks, and the notice you give before a model or\n  prompt change.\n- **What you can skip.** Contract law and credit negotiation tactics. Know the allowance table, the\n  dependency-ceiling product and why quality stays out of the uptime percentage.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Enterprise support or operations agents.** The customer routes tickets or back-office tasks through\n  the agent and procurement asks for availability, latency and Sev1 response terms before go-live.\n- **Agent platforms serving internal teams.** Product teams building on your shared agent runtime need a\n  written run-level availability and latency target to plan their own commitments.\n- **Choosing a model provider or fallback.** The dependency-ceiling arithmetic decides whether a second\n  provider or region is required for the tier sales wants to sell.\n- **Model and prompt upgrades.** The model-change notice clause sets how far ahead you announce a swap\n  and whether the customer can pin a version.","mini_project":"**Run-level SLA report for a toy agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** compute a contract-style monthly attainment for an agent from its own run traces.\n- **Steps:** build a small tool-calling agent with two fake tools that fail at configurable rates; run it\n  a few hundred times with and without one retry and with a step cap; log one record per run (outcome,\n  steps, duration, failing dependency); compute run success, p95 run latency, budget used against a 99.5\n  percent allowance and the credit tier from the calculator above.\n- **Done when:** you have a one-page report comparing per-call and per-run availability, showing what\n  retries buy and cost in latency, and naming the SLA number you would sign for this agent."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    D[\"Model, tool, vector store SLAs\"] --> C[\"Ceiling: own x product of deps\"]\n    C --> S[\"Internal SLO below ceiling\"]\n    S --> A[\"Signed SLA below SLO\"]\n    I[\"Incident\"] --> E{\"Excluded by contract?\"}\n    E -->|\"yes, with evidence\"| R[\"Logged, no budget used\"]\n    E -->|\"no\"| B[\"Month-to-date budget\"]\n    B -->|\"budget low\"| F[\"Freeze risky changes\"]\n    B --> M[\"Month-end attainment report\"]\n    A --> M\n    M -->|\"below target\"| CR[\"Credit if customer claims\"]","caption":"Read it top to bottom: the agent's dependencies set the ceiling, the ceiling bounds the SLO, the SLO\nbounds the SLA, and every incident (a provider outage, a broken tool, a bad prompt deploy) flows into the\nmonthly attainment and credit decision."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between an SLA, an SLO and an SLI, and why keep all three?","a":"- **SLI is the measurement,** for example the share of valid requests answered without error within the\n  latency bound, computed at a stated point such as your load balancer.\n- **SLO is the internal target** on that SLI, and the SLA is the external contract with remedies attached.\n- **Why three:** the SLO sits stricter than the SLA so alerts fire and the team acts before a miss\n  becomes credits, and the SLI definition is what both sides must compute identically."},{"q":"A customer's monitoring shows 98.9 percent for the month and yours shows 99.7 percent. How do you resolve it?","a":"- **Go back to the contract's metric definition:** measurement point, bad-minute rule and which requests\n  count as valid. The gap usually lives in one of those three.\n- **Recompute with the contract's own query** and compare minute by minute; a sustained partial error\n  rate or client-side timeouts often explain the difference.\n- **Fix it for next month:** tighten the definition, share the data source both sides can inspect, and\n  attach SLA-impact evidence to each incident."},{"q":"When would you refuse to exclude upstream model provider outages, and when would you push for it?","a":"- **Accept owning it** when you chose the provider, because customers reasonably say your vendor choice\n  is your risk; then you need a tested fallback and a lower committed number.\n- **Push for the exclusion** when the customer chose or mandated that provider, because you cannot route\n  around their decision.\n- **Either way,** price the risk: the provider credit you receive is far smaller than the credit you owe."},{"q":"Sales wants to sign 99.9 percent. The path is provider 99.9, vector database 99.95, and your service has historically run at about 99.9. What do you say?","a":"- **Compute the ceiling:** 0.999 × 0.9995 × 0.999 ≈ 99.75 percent, so 99.9 would be breached in an\n  ordinary month.\n- **Translate it into minutes:** 99.9 allows 43.2 minutes a month, less than one human\n  page-diagnose-rollback cycle.\n- **Offer options:** sign 99.5 percent now, or 99.9 only after a tested cross-region or cross-provider\n  fallback raises the provider term."},{"q":"How do you handle answer quality and latency for an LLM agent in an SLA?","a":"- **Quality** goes into acceptance criteria, recurring eval reports and a regression-remediation\n  commitment, not an uptime-style percentage neither side measures the same way.\n- **Latency** is defined as time to first token or per-request latency under a token cap, because output\n  length drives total latency.\n- **Model changes** get a notice clause and, where possible, version pinning, so a model swap is not a\n  silent contract violation."},{"q":"Your agent makes about seven model and tool calls per run. How do you define and defend its availability SLI?","a":"- **Define the unit as a run:** a valid user task that ends in an answer or an explicit handoff within the\n  wall-clock bound counts as good; errors, step-cap exhaustion and timeouts count as bad.\n- **Compute the ceiling per run:** multiply per-call success rates by calls per run, then show how retries\n  and a fallback model lift it, assuming independent failures and naming the correlated ones.\n- **Defend it with traces:** one record per run with the failing dependency, so exclusions and credits are\n  argued from evidence."}],"tradeoffs":["**Tighter SLA vs cost.** It wins enterprise deals and premium pricing, but each step multiplies the redundancy and on-call cost; it flips to worthwhile only when the price uplift exceeds that recurring cost.","**Excluding upstream provider outages vs trust.** It protects your margin, but customers read it as you not owning your architecture; it flips toward acceptable when the customer chose or mandated that provider.","**Request-based vs probe-minute measurement.** Requests are fairer to partial outages but need logs both sides trust; it flips to probe-based when the customer cannot see your logs or traffic is too sparse for per-minute error rates to mean anything.","**Resolution-time commitments vs control.** They reassure buyers, but you cannot control unknown root causes; they only become safe for narrow, well-understood failure classes with a documented workaround.","**Automatic vs claim-based credits.** Automatic credits build trust and avoid claim disputes, but cost money on breaches nobody noticed; claim-based credits are cheaper but make every breach a negotiation.","**Retries and fallbacks vs run latency.** Retrying failed tool and model calls lifts run-level availability sharply, but every retry spends latency budget; it flips against retries when the latency bound is tight or failures are correlated, where a fallback model or region is the better spend."],"pitfalls":["**Signing the number sales promised without computing the dependency ceiling.** Symptom: breaching in a month where no single component missed its own SLA.","**Defining the metric vaguely (\"the service is available\").** Symptom: a month-end dispute where your dashboard says 99.7 percent and the customer's monitoring says 98.9 percent.","**Promising 24/7 response with no funded rotation.** Symptom: a Sev1 acknowledged hours late because the only person on call was asleep or on a plane.","**Treating the SLA target as the alerting threshold.** Symptom: learning about a breach from the customer's credit claim instead of from an internal SLO alert days earlier.","**Leaving the \"bad minute\" and valid-request rules undefined.** Symptom: a month with a sustained partial error rate that your probe calls 100 percent available while the customer's users saw failures.","**Changing the model or prompt without the notice the contract requires.** Symptom: a customer escalating a \"silent regression\" that is also a contractual violation.","**Measuring the agent per model call.** Symptom: the dashboard shows 99.9 percent while customers see failed runs every day, because one run makes many calls. Fix: compute the SLI per run from traces.","**Unbounded agent loops under a latency promise.** Symptom: p95 run latency breached by a few runs that looped 30 steps. Fix: step, token and wall-clock caps with a graceful handoff."],"when":"- **Use when:** any production agent deployment a customer pays for and depends on, and internal agent\n  platforms other teams build on.\n- **Pilots and proofs of concept:** skip formal credits, but still write down response expectations so\n  the pilot does not set an unspoken bar.\n- **Before signing:** compute the dependency ceiling per agent run (calls per run times per-call\n  rates), confirm the on-call staffing behind every support\n  commitment, and check that the metric can be computed from data both sides can see.\n- **While operating:** track SLA attainment monthly, alert on the stricter internal SLO, and review the\n  contract whenever architecture or providers change.\n- **Interviews:** expect \"how would you set the SLA for this system?\" and answer with the chain\n  arithmetic.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the SLI exactly as the contract defines it, builds the monthly attainment report, and wires internal SLO burn alerts that fire well before the contractual threshold.","architect":"Owns the gap between the dependency ceiling and the committed number, deciding which redundancy, fallback provider or region the promised SLA actually requires.","fde":"Negotiates exclusions, measurement points and model-change notice with the customer's procurement and operations teams, translating their fears into terms you can honestly meet."},"check":{"q":"A customer asks for 99.95 percent monthly availability and a two-hour resolution time for Sev1. Your\npath depends on one LLM provider (99.9 percent SLA) and a managed database (99.99 percent). What do you offer?","a":"First the ceiling: 0.999 × 0.9999 ≈ 0.9989 before any of my own failures, so 99.95 percent cannot\nbe backed on this architecture: the provider's own contract permits more downtime than my whole budget,\nand signing it means betting on the provider beating its SLA every month. I would offer 99.5 percent, or 99.9\npercent only if they fund a tested secondary provider and region, which raises the provider term close\nto one. On support, I commit to a Sev1 response time with 24/7 coverage that our rotation can staff,\nplus a workaround or status update cadence, but not a two-hour resolution, because root-cause time is\nunbounded. I would also add a model-change notice clause and define latency per request under a token cap."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should the internal SLO be stricter than the external SLA?","options":["Because regulators require internal targets to exceed contractual ones","Because the gap gives time to detect and fix degradation before it becomes a contract breach","Because customers measure availability more leniently than providers do","Because service credits are calculated from the internal SLO"],"answer":1,"explanation":"If the alerting target equals the contract, you learn about problems only once you owe credits. A stricter SLO burns first and gives the team room to act; regulation and credit formulas do not require it."},{"q":"In a support SLA, which commitment is usually safe to make for every severity level?","options":["A guaranteed resolution time for each severity","A guaranteed root-cause report within one hour","A response time for a qualified human to acknowledge and begin work","A guarantee that no Sev1 incident will recur"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Response time is within your control if the rotation is staffed. Resolution depends on an unknown root cause, so promising it per severity converts hard incidents into automatic breaches."},{"q":"Your provider SLA is 99.9 percent and you sign 99.9 percent with your customer. What is the main flaw?","options":["Provider SLAs are measured quarterly, so the windows cannot match","Your service also depends on other components and your own code, so its ceiling is below 99.9 percent","Customer SLAs must always be at least one nine looser than provider SLAs","Credits from the provider will fully cover the credits you owe"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Serial dependencies multiply, so any additional failure source pushes you below the provider's number. There is no fixed one-nine rule, and provider credits are usually far smaller than what you owe."}]},{"key":"delivery-ownership/system-reliability","sources":["Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering","Nygard, 2018 — Release It! (2nd ed.)"],"why":"- **Delivered AI systems fail differently from demos.** They fail at 3 a.m. when a provider rate-limits\n  a region, the day a vendor silently updates a model, when one tenant's batch job starves everyone\n  else, or when an agent's tenth tool call times out and leaves half a workflow done.\n- **Tests do not catch these.** Not \"it works on my machine\", and mostly not unit tests, *because* the\n  failures come from load, time, dependencies and change rather than from logic errors.\n- **\"Add redundancy everywhere\" fails twice.**\n  - It is expensive and still misses the dominant cause: postmortem experience across the industry,\n    Google's SRE writing included, finds a large share of outages is triggered by changes (deploys,\n    config, prompts, model versions), and redundant copies running the same bad change fail together.\n  - It optimises only one lever. A system that fails rarely but takes four hours to diagnose each time\n    can be less available than one that fails weekly and rolls back in five minutes.\n- **Someone has to own the whole picture:** which failures matter, which lever is cheapest to pull\n  next, and whether the system is getting better or worse. Without that owner, reliability work happens\n  only after incidents and only on whatever the last incident touched.\n- **Scope.** This lesson is the owner's view; the patterns themselves (redundancy, circuit breakers,\n  bulkheads) are taught in the reliability module.","how":"### The four levers and the impact formula\n- **Availability over time** ≈ MTBF / (MTBF + MTTR): mean uptime between failures over the whole cycle\n  of working plus being broken.\n- **Convention caveat.** Strictly that numerator is MTTF, mean time to failure. If your MTBF already\n  includes repair time, the formula is (MTBF − MTTR) / MTBF. Conventions differ, so say which you use.\n- **Split MTTR** into time to detect, time to diagnose and time to recover, add blast radius (the share\n  of users affected), and you have every lever you can pull. They combine into one cost function the\n  owner can rank work by:\n\n```\nuser_impact_minutes per period ≈ Σ over incidents of\n    (time_to_detect + time_to_diagnose + time_to_recover) × blast_radius_share\nexpected for a failure mode ≈ frequency × mean_duration × mean_blast_radius\n```\n\n- **The product form is the point.** Each term is a separate lever; halving any one term halves the\n  impact, so the cheapest term to halve is where the next sprint goes.\n\n### Lever 1: fail less often (raise MTBF)\n- **Change safety beats hardware:** canary and shadow releases, evaluation gates on prompt and model\n  changes, pinned model versions, config review.\n- **Start here,** *because* change causes most incidents, so this is usually where the first effort\n  pays off.\n\n### Lever 2: fail smaller (reduce blast radius)\n- **Contain the width:** per-tenant quotas, bulkheads between workloads, feature flags, cell or region\n  isolation. A failure that hits 2 percent of users for an hour costs far less error budget than one\n  that hits everyone.\n- **Graceful degradation shrinks the depth.** Serve a worse answer instead of none: a smaller or\n  secondary model, a cached response, retrieval-only results without generation, a read-only mode,\n  *because* users tolerate degraded far better than down.\n- **Stop the spread.** Timeouts, retry budgets, circuit breakers and load shedding stop one slow\n  dependency from exhausting threads, connections or quota for everything else, *because* the usual way\n  a small failure becomes a big one is queueing behind it.\n\n### Lever 3: notice sooner (reduce detection time)\n- **Alert on symptoms users feel:** SLO burn, failed tasks, quality-eval pass rate on sampled traffic,\n  not CPU.\n- **Quality monitoring is decisive for AI,** *because* it is the difference between detecting a\n  regression in an hour or from a customer email a week later.\n\n### Lever 4: recover faster (reduce diagnose and recover time)\n- **One-step rollback** for code, prompts and model versions.\n- **Runbooks, and traces** that connect a user's request to every model and tool call.\n- **Automatic failover** for failures that need sub-minute recovery, *because* humans cannot act that fast.\n\n### Composition arithmetic\n| Shape | Formula | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Serial dependencies | multiply: ∏ aᵢ | four components at 99.99, 99.9, 99.95 and 99.9 percent succeed about 99.74 percent of the time |\n| Redundant alternatives | 1 − ∏(1 − aᵢ) | only if failures are independent |\n| Multi-step agent task | per-step success to the power of steps | ten steps at 99 percent each complete about 90 percent of tasks |\n\n- **Independence is the trap.** Shared regions, shared quotas and shared bad configs violate it, so real\n  redundancy gains are smaller than the formula says.\n\n### AI-specific failure classes\n- **Dependency failures**: provider outages, rate limits (HTTP 429), regional quota exhaustion.\n- **Silent quality failures**: a model update, a retrieval index gone stale, a prompt edit; every request\n  returns 200 and the answers get worse.\n- **Partial side effects**: an agent fails mid-workflow; reliability then requires idempotent tools and\n  resumable state, not just retries.\n- **Nondeterminism**: the same input sometimes fails, so \"could not reproduce\" is not evidence of a fix.\n\n### The four levers inside an agent loop\n| Lever | What it looks like in the agent |\n|---|---|\n| Fail less often | eval gate on prompt, tool-schema and model changes; pinned model snapshot; validated tool arguments |\n| Fail smaller | per-step timeout, step cap per run, per-tenant quota, degraded mode (smaller model, retrieval-only answer, handoff) |\n| Notice sooner | task-completion rate and sampled quality evals on agent traces, not only HTTP status |\n| Recover faster | checkpointed run state, idempotent tools so a resumed run does not repeat writes, one-step prompt rollback |\n\n- **Resumable beats restartable.** A run that checkpoints after each completed tool call can resume at\n  step 7 instead of repeating steps 1-6, *because* repeating them costs tokens and, for writes, creates\n  duplicates.\n- **Retry only what is transient.** Timeouts, HTTP 429 and 5xx are worth a bounded retry; a tool that\n  rejects the arguments the model produced needs the error fed back to the model or a stop, not a\n  blind retry.\n\n### The ownership loop\n1. **Define SLOs**, including a quality SLO (for example, the pass rate of an automated evaluation on\n   sampled production traffic), *because* availability alone cannot see the silent failures above.\n2. **Agree an error-budget policy in advance**: what happens when the budget is spent (change freeze\n   except fixes, reliability work moves to the top of the queue). Agreeing it before the crisis is what\n   stops the argument from being re-litigated every time a launch is due.\n3. **Review weekly**: budget burn, incidents, and the per-incident split of detect, diagnose and recover\n   time, *because* incident count alone hides which lever is failing.\n4. **Rank failure modes** from incidents and failure analysis with the impact formula, and fund the\n   top items, not the most recent ones.\n5. **Verify** with game days, failover drills and chaos tests, *because* a recovery path that has not\n   been exercised should be assumed broken. Then repeat.\n\nBefore launch, a production readiness review checks each lever explicitly.","example":"### Composition and recovery arithmetic\nThe arithmetic an owner runs before deciding where the next sprint of reliability work goes. All\ncomponent figures are illustrative, not vendor numbers.\n\n```python\nfrom math import prod\n\ndef serial(*a):   return prod(a)                        # every component must work\ndef parallel(*a): return 1 - prod(1 - x for x in a)     # any one suffices; assumes independence\ndef availability(mtbf_h, mttr_h): return mtbf_h / (mtbf_h + mttr_h)\n\ngateway, llm, vectors, app = 0.9999, 0.999, 0.9995, 0.999\nbackup_llm = 0.995\nprint(f\"single provider: {serial(gateway, llm, vectors, app):.2%}\")                      # 99.74%\nprint(f\"with fallback:   {serial(gateway, parallel(llm, backup_llm), vectors, app):.2%}\") # 99.84%\n\n# The app's own term: one incident per month (~720 h) at two recovery speeds\nfor mttr in (2.0, 0.25):\n    print(f\"MTTR {mttr:>4} h -> {availability(720, mttr):.3%}\")   # 99.723% vs 99.965%\n\n# Agent task of 10 tool calls, 1% transient failure each, without and with one retry\np, n = 0.01, 10\nprint(f\"no retry {(1 - p) ** n:.1%}, one retry {(1 - p ** 2) ** n:.2%}\")  # 90.4% vs 99.90%\n```\n\nThree conclusions follow:\n- **The fallback provider buys only a tenth of a percent,** *because* the other serial terms now dominate.\n- **Cutting recovery from two hours to fifteen minutes** (one-step rollback plus a runbook) moves the\n  app's own term far more.\n- **Retries rescue the agent only if failures are transient and the tools are idempotent;** a\n  deterministic bug fails both attempts.\n\n### Worked scenario: ranking failure modes by expected impact\nA quarter's incident history, reduced to per-month averages with the impact formula\n`frequency × mean_duration × mean_blast_radius`. Figures are illustrative.\n\n| Failure mode | Per month | Mean minutes | Users hit | Impact (full-outage-equivalent minutes) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Bad prompt deploy | 1 | 180 | 100% | 180 |\n| Stale retrieval index | 1 | 600 | 10% | 60 |\n| Provider region outage | 0.5 | 30 | 100% | 15 |\n| Tenant batch starves others | 2 | 20 | 30% | 12 |\n\n- **Fund the top row first.** Prompt deploys produce more impact than the other three combined, and they\n  are self-inflicted, so they are cheap to fix.\n- **Pick the term inside it.** If incident timelines show about 150 of the 180 minutes were detection\n  (a bad prompt still returns 200s), sampled quality evals that alert within 15 minutes cut that row to\n  about 45 user-minutes.\n- **A second provider is last,** *because* it addresses the 15-minute row while costing the most.\n\n### Agent loop with retry budget, idempotent writes and checkpoints\nA minimal sketch of the recover-faster and fail-smaller levers inside one agent run. `call_tool`,\n`save_checkpoint` and the error classes stand in for your own orchestrator and tool client.\n\n```python\nimport time, uuid\n\nTRANSIENT = (TimeoutError, ConnectionError)   # plus your client's 429 / 5xx error types\nMAX_STEPS, MAX_RETRIES = 12, 2\n\ndef run_agent(task, plan_next_step, call_tool, save_checkpoint, state=None):\n    state = state or {\"run_id\": str(uuid.uuid4()), \"step\": 0, \"history\": []}\n    while state[\"step\"] < MAX_STEPS:\n        action = plan_next_step(task, state[\"history\"])      # model call\n        if action[\"type\"] == \"final\":\n            return {\"status\": \"done\", \"answer\": action[\"text\"]}\n        # same key on every retry or resume, so the tool server can drop duplicates\n        key = f'{state[\"run_id\"]}:{state[\"step\"]}'\n        for attempt in range(MAX_RETRIES + 1):\n            try:\n                result = call_tool(action[\"tool\"], action[\"args\"], idempotency_key=key, timeout=20)\n                break\n            except TRANSIENT:\n                if attempt == MAX_RETRIES:\n                    return {\"status\": \"handoff\", \"reason\": \"tool unavailable\", \"state\": state}\n                time.sleep(2 ** attempt)                       # backoff; add jitter in production\n        state[\"history\"].append({\"action\": action, \"result\": result})\n        state[\"step\"] += 1\n        save_checkpoint(state)                                 # resume here, not from step 0\n    return {\"status\": \"handoff\", \"reason\": \"step cap\", \"state\": state}\n```\n\n- **What it buys:** a 1 percent transient failure per call becomes about 0.01 percent with one retry\n  (this sketch allows two), the 90.4 percent versus 99.90 percent shown above.\n- **What it does not buy:** a deterministic bug fails every attempt, and without the idempotency key a\n  retried write (a refund, a ticket update) may run twice.\n- **Persist the planned action before calling a write tool.** On resume this sketch re-plans the step,\n  and the model may choose a different action under the same key, which the tool side would wrongly drop\n  as a duplicate. Checkpoint the action first and replay it, so the key always names one action.","patterns":["**Pinned model versions with a promotion step** — when calling a hosted model, reference a dated model snapshot rather than a floating alias, and promote a new version only after it passes the eval set on a canary. Monitor quality-eval pass rate per model version.","**Timeouts, retry budgets and circuit breakers on every provider call** — set an explicit per-call timeout, retry only transient errors such as HTTP 429 and 5xx with exponential backoff and jitter, honour a `Retry-After` header when the provider sends one, and open a breaker after repeated failures. Monitor retry rate, breaker state changes and queue depth.","**Degraded-mode kill switch** — when a dependency fails, flip a feature flag that routes to a secondary model, cached answers or retrieval-only results. Rehearse the flip in game days. Monitor traffic share in degraded mode and its quality score.","**Quality SLO on sampled traffic** — run an automated evaluation on a fixed sample of production requests every hour and page when the pass rate drops below the SLO. Monitor pass rate per route, judge disagreement, and eval spend.","**Per-tenant quotas and bulkheads** — give batch and interactive workloads separate concurrency pools and rate limits so one tenant cannot exhaust shared provider quota. Monitor per-tenant quota utilisation and rejections.","**Detect, diagnose, recover timestamps in every postmortem** — record the three durations as fields, not prose, so the weekly review can see which lever is failing. Monitor their medians per quarter.","**Idempotency keys and checkpoints for agent runs** — derive a key from run id and step for every tool call that writes, have the tool side drop repeats, and persist run state after each step so a crashed or timed-out run resumes. Monitor duplicate-write rejections, resumed runs and task-completion rate."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Per-step failures compound: ten tool calls at 99 percent each finish\n  about 90 percent of tasks. An agent that fails mid-workflow also leaves half-done side effects, so\n  reliability is designed into the loop, not bolted on afterwards.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Tool-call timeouts and retry budgets, idempotency keys on tools\n  that write, checkpointed run state your orchestrator can resume, fallback models behind a feature flag,\n  pinned model snapshots and quality evals on sampled agent traces.\n- **What you control.** Which errors you retry, how many steps a run may take, whether a tool is safe to\n  call twice, what the agent does when a dependency is down, and how fast you can roll back a prompt or\n  model.\n- **What you can skip.** Hardware redundancy theory and the MTBF versus MTTF naming debate beyond knowing\n  conventions differ. The impact formula and composition arithmetic are what you use.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Agents that write to customer systems.** Ticketing, refunds or CRM updates need idempotent tools and\n  resumable runs so a crash or retry never duplicates a business action.\n- **Agents on a single hosted model provider.** Rate limits and regional outages are the common\n  dependency failure; timeouts, retry budgets and a degraded-mode flag keep runs finishing.\n- **Prompt and model upgrades.** Most incidents are change-induced, so eval gates, canaries and one-step\n  rollback are the first reliability investment for an agent team.\n- **Quarterly reliability planning.** Ranking failure modes by frequency × duration × blast radius\n  decides whether the next sprint goes to detection, rollback or a second provider.","mini_project":"**Make a flaky agent finish its tasks (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** raise the task-completion rate of a 10-step tool-calling agent without duplicating writes.\n- **Steps:** build an agent whose tools fail transiently 1 percent of the time and whose one write tool\n  records every call; run 500 tasks and measure completion; add a bounded retry with backoff, an\n  idempotency key per run and step, and a checkpoint after each step; inject crashes mid-run and resume\n  from the checkpoint.\n- **Done when:** completion climbs from about 90 percent toward 99.9 percent, the write tool shows zero\n  duplicate effects after resumes, and you can name which failures retries could not fix."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"Prompt change or tool fault\"] --> F[\"Failure in production\"]\n    F --> D[\"Detected\"]\n    D --> G[\"Diagnosed\"]\n    G --> R[\"Recovered\"]\n    L1[\"Lever 1: eval gates, canaries\"] -.->|\"fewer failures\"| C\n    L2[\"Lever 2: bulkheads, degradation\"] -.->|\"smaller blast radius\"| F\n    L3[\"Lever 3: symptom and quality alerts\"] -.->|\"faster detect\"| D\n    L4[\"Lever 4: rollback, checkpoints, traces\"] -.->|\"faster diagnose, recover\"| G\n    R --> W[\"Weekly review: rank by impact\"]\n    W -->|\"fund cheapest lever\"| L1","caption":"Follow one incident through its life; each dotted \"Lever\" box points at the stage it shortens or\nshrinks, and the weekly review at the bottom decides what gets funded next."},"interview":[{"q":"What does \"reliable\" mean for an LLM agent, and how is it different from \"available\"?","a":"- **Reliable means doing the right thing for users over time,** which includes answer quality, not just\n  returning responses.\n- **Available is narrower:** a system can serve HTTP 200 on every request while a model update or stale\n  index makes answers wrong, so it is up but unreliable.\n- **So** you need a quality SLO on sampled traffic alongside the availability SLO."},{"q":"An agent completes only about 90 percent of its 10-step tasks. How do you debug and improve that?","a":"- **Do the arithmetic first:** 99 percent per step over ten steps is about 90 percent per task, so the\n  per-step failure rate is the target.\n- **Classify the failures** from traces: transient (timeouts, 429s) versus deterministic bugs, because\n  retries help only the first kind.\n- **Fix in order:** idempotent tools and resumable state, then a bounded retry, which moves the task to\n  about 99.9 percent for transient failures."},{"q":"When would you not add a second LLM provider for redundancy?","a":"- **When the other serial terms dominate:** in the worked example the fallback adds only about a tenth\n  of a percent.\n- **When recovery time is the bigger lever:** cutting MTTR from two hours to fifteen minutes moves the\n  app's own term much more.\n- **When failures are correlated:** a shared region, quota or client bug takes both paths down together,\n  and an untested fallback is likely broken anyway."},{"q":"Your service has one incident a month. What availability does a two-hour versus a fifteen-minute MTTR give?","a":"- **Use MTBF / (MTBF + MTTR)** with about 720 hours between failures.\n- **Two hours** gives about 99.72 percent; **fifteen minutes** gives about 99.97 percent.\n- **Lesson:** at this failure rate, recovery speed is worth far more than another nine of component\n  redundancy."},{"q":"How do you decide where the next sprint of reliability work goes?","a":"- **Rank failure modes by expected impact:** frequency × mean duration × mean blast radius, from incident\n  history and failure analysis.\n- **Inside the top mode,** look at the detect, diagnose and recover split and halve the cheapest term,\n  because halving any term halves the impact.\n- **Let the error-budget policy decide timing,** agreed in advance so it is not re-argued at each launch."},{"q":"Your agent crashed at step 6 of 9 after creating a ticket and issuing a refund. How should a retry behave?","a":"- **Resume, do not restart:** load the checkpoint written after step 6 and continue from step 7, so the\n  model does not re-plan and repeat earlier writes.\n- **Make writes idempotent:** each tool call carries a key built from run id and step, so a replayed\n  refund is rejected as a duplicate by the tool side.\n- **Escalate if unsure:** if the last write's outcome is unknown, reconcile against the target system or\n  hand off to a human rather than guess."}],"tradeoffs":["**Redundancy vs cost and rot.** It raises availability for independent failures, but doubles cost and adds failover paths that rot unless exercised; it flips to worthwhile once recovery time, not failure rate, is already small.","**Strict change gates vs delivery speed.** Evals, canaries and approvals cut change-induced incidents, but slow delivery; loosen them when the error budget is healthy and tighten them when it is being burned.","**Quality monitoring vs spend and noise.** Monitoring sampled traffic catches silent regressions, but costs evaluation spend and judge noise; it flips to essential as soon as outputs feed decisions without human review.","**Automatic vs manual failover.** Automatic failover recovers in seconds, but a flapping health check can cause the outage it guards against; prefer manual failover where minutes of recovery are contractually acceptable.","**Agent retries vs latency and cost.** Bounded retries lift task completion sharply for transient failures, but every retried model call adds tokens and seconds; it flips against retrying when failures are deterministic or the run is already near its latency budget, where a handoff is better."],"pitfalls":["**Measuring reliability only as HTTP success.** Symptom: green dashboards during a week of customer complaints about wrong answers after a provider's model update.","**Adding retries everywhere without idempotency or budgets.** Symptom: duplicate side effects and retry storms that turn a brief provider blip into a long outage.","**Building a fallback or degraded path nobody tests.** Symptom: discovering during the first real outage that the backup provider's credentials expired, its prompt format was never adapted, or its quota cannot absorb full production traffic.","**Fixing whatever the last incident touched.** Symptom: the same failure class recurring across postmortems while the team feels busy.","**Assuming failures are independent when computing redundancy.** Symptom: both \"redundant\" providers failing together because they share a region, quota or upstream.","**Restarting failed agent runs from step 0.** Symptom: duplicate tickets or refunds and doubled token spend after a crash. Fix: checkpoint after each step and pass an idempotency key on writes.","**Tracking only HTTP errors for an agent.** Symptom: runs that loop to the step cap or end without an answer count as successes. Fix: alert on task-completion rate per agent and tool."],"when":"- **Start on day one:** reliability ownership begins the day an agent has users who depend on it, not\n  after the first outage; idempotent tools and step caps are cheapest to add before the first write tool\n  ships.\n- **Before launch:** run a readiness review against the four levers: how changes are gated, how failures\n  are contained, which symptom alerts exist, and how long rollback takes.\n- **In operation:** let the error budget and the ranked failure list decide what to fund, and measure\n  MTTR and detection time per incident, not just incident count.\n- **Pilots:** do not gold-plate with multi-region redundancy; do insist even there on rollback and\n  quality monitoring.\n- **Interviews:** reliability questions reward reasoning about which lever is cheapest for the given\n  failure profile, with the composition arithmetic shown.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the mechanisms that move each lever: idempotent tools, retry budgets, one-step rollback for prompts and models, and traces that make a failed task diagnosable.","architect":"Decides where redundancy, isolation and fallback belong by running the composition arithmetic, and sets the reliability targets each component must meet.","fde":"Learns how the customer's network, identity provider and data systems fail, because in their environment those dependencies, not your code, often dominate the reliability budget."},"check":{"q":"Your assistant had six incidents last quarter: two provider outages of 30 minutes, three bad prompt\ndeploys each taking about three hours to notice and roll back, and one stale index. Where do you invest first?","a":"The prompt deploys dominate: roughly nine hours of degraded service versus one hour from the\nprovider, and they are self-inflicted, so they are cheap to fix. The formula also says where in their\nduration to cut: I would expect most of those three hours to be detection, because a bad prompt returns 200s, so I would confirm that from the incident timelines before choosing the fix. I would add an evaluation gate and a\ncanary for prompt changes to cut their frequency, quality monitoring on sampled traffic to detect a bad\nprompt within minutes, and one-step prompt rollback to shrink recovery. The stale index points to the\nsame detection gap and needs a freshness check. A second provider is worth less until those are done,\nbecause it addresses the smallest slice of downtime."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which change most directly reduces MTTR rather than failure frequency?","options":["Adding an evaluation gate before prompt changes reach production","Making prompt and model versions revertible in one step","Pinning the model version instead of using a floating alias","Running a canary on 5 percent of traffic before full rollout"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Recovery time shrinks when reverting is fast and safe. Gates, pinning and canaries prevent or contain bad changes, which raises MTBF or reduces blast radius rather than shortening recovery."},{"q":"Two redundant LLM providers each claim 99.9 percent. Why might the pair deliver far less than 1 − 0.001²?","options":["Because SLA percentages cannot be multiplied","Because the formula applies only to hardware failures","Because failures can be correlated through shared regions, quotas, client code or configuration","Because failover always takes longer than the outage"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The parallel formula assumes independence. A shared cloud region, a common client library bug or a bad shared config takes both paths down at once, so real gains are smaller."},{"q":"Why is answer quality part of system reliability for an LLM product?","options":["Because quality metrics are required in every SLA","Because a system can return successful responses while users receive wrong answers, which is a failure to them","Because low quality always causes HTTP errors eventually","Because quality monitoring replaces availability monitoring"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Reliability means doing the right thing for users. Silent quality regressions produce 200 responses, so they need their own SLO and monitoring alongside availability, not instead of it."}]},{"key":"delivery-ownership/technical-documentation","sources":["Official docs: Diátaxis documentation framework","Nygard, 2011 — Documenting Architecture Decisions","Mitchell et al., 2019 — Model Cards for Model Reporting"],"why":"- **Systems outlive their builders' attention.** The forward-deployed engineer rotates to the next\n  customer, the architect changes teams, and the customer's operations team inherits a pipeline at 2 a.m.\n  with an error message they have never seen. Every unwritten answer becomes a page to someone who has\n  moved on, or a guess.\n- **The same questions repeat.** Security reviewers, auditors and new team members ask them again and\n  again; without documents, senior people answer them in meetings forever.\n- **The naive approaches fail.**\n  - \"The code is the documentation\" answers what the system does, never why, what was rejected, or what\n    to do when it breaks.\n  - One giant wiki page mixing tutorial, configuration reference and design rationale serves nobody: the\n    newcomer drowns in reference tables and the on-call engineer cannot find the one command they need.\n  - Documentation written once at the end decays immediately, *because* the system keeps changing and\n    nothing forces the docs to follow.\n- **Drift is structural, not laziness.** The author pays the cost of updating a document and a different\n  person, later, pays the cost of it being wrong. Without a mechanism that puts the update at the moment\n  of change, drift is the default. A stale doc is worse than none: it is trusted, and it is wrong.\n- **AI systems sharpen this.** Behaviour depends on artefacts that are not obviously code: the model\n  version, the prompt, the retrieval index, the evaluation set. When output changes, the first question\n  is which of these changed, and only documentation and registries can answer it.","how":"### One reader, one job per document\nThe Diátaxis framework separates documentation into four types *because* readers arrive in four\ndifferent modes, and mixing them makes each worse:\n\n| Type | Reader and mode | What it contains |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Tutorials** | a newcomer, learning by doing | a guaranteed-to-work path from nothing to a first result; no options, no edge cases |\n| **How-to guides** | a competent reader with a goal | \"Rotate the provider API key\", \"add a new tenant\": steps, preconditions, how to verify success |\n| **Reference** | lookup | configuration keys, API endpoints, environment variables, error codes |\n| **Explanation** | understanding | architecture, design rationale, trade-offs and constraints |\n\n- **Generate reference** from the source of truth wherever possible (OpenAPI, config schemas), *because*\n  hand-written reference drifts first.\n\n### The delivery document set\nFor a system handed to a customer or another team, the minimum set usually maps onto those types:\n- **Architecture overview** (explanation): components, data flows, trust boundaries, external\n  dependencies. Security review starts here, so draw the data flow, not just the boxes.\n- **Architecture decision records** (explanation): one short record per significant decision with\n  context, options and consequences, *because* six months later \"why not just use X?\" is the question.\n- **Runbooks** (how-to): one per alert, in a fixed order:\n  1. symptom and how to confirm it;\n  2. user impact;\n  3. the first safe action, usually rollback or failover, *because* it stabilises before anyone\n     understands the cause;\n  4. diagnosis steps with expected output;\n  5. a \"stop and escalate if\" line naming who to call.\n- **API and configuration reference**, generated where possible.\n- **AI system card** (explanation plus reference): model and version, prompt versions, retrieval\n  sources, evaluation sets and latest results, intended use, out-of-scope uses and known failure modes.\n  This borrows the model-card idea and extends it to the whole system.\n- **Operations and support guide**: who owns what, escalation paths, SLAs, how to roll back.\n\n### What agents add to the set\n- **Tool reference doubles as model context.** Each tool's name, description and parameter docs are sent\n  to the model and decide when and how it calls the tool, *because* the model has nothing else to go on.\n  Write them like a how-to for a competent reader: what it does, when to use it, what not to pass.\n- **Agent system card.** Extend the AI system card with the tool list and each tool's permissions (read\n  or write, which systems), step and token caps, human-approval points and the handoff path, *because*\n  security reviewers ask \"what can this agent do on its own?\" first.\n- **Agent runbooks.** Write one per agent alert: runaway loops hitting the step cap, a tool error spike,\n  completion-rate drop, duplicated writes. The first safe action is usually a prompt or model rollback or\n  disabling one tool behind a flag.\n- **Decision records for loop design.** Record why a step cap is 12, why a tool needs approval or why a\n  retry exists, so a new owner does not \"simplify\" a guard that prevents a known failure.\n- **Instruction files for coding agents.** If agents work in the repository, a short instruction file at\n  its root (build, test and convention commands) is reference written for an agent reader; keep it under\n  the same freshness checks.\n\n### Docs as code\n1. **Keep documents in the repository,** change them in the same pull request as the code, and review\n   them with it.\n2. **Ask in the pull-request template** \"which docs does this change invalidate?\", *because* review is\n   where staleness is cheapest to catch.\n3. **Add CI checks:** broken links, executable code samples, generated reference rebuilt on each build,\n   and freshness rules mapping docs to the code they cover.\n4. **Give every document an owner and a review date;** an unowned document is a stale document waiting\n   to happen.\n\n### Versioned with what is deployed\n- **Describe the version the reader is running.** If a customer runs release 2.3 and the docs describe\n  2.5, the runbook's commands may not exist yet.\n- **Tag docs with the release that built them** and publish per-version reference.\n- **Pull versions, do not type them.** Put the deployed model, prompt and index versions in the system\n  card by pulling them from the registry or deployment manifest, *because* typed version strings are the\n  first thing to go stale.\n\n### Testing documentation\nA document is only verified when someone other than its author uses it to do the job:\n- **Tutorial test:** a new team member follows it from a clean machine and records each place they stalled.\n- **Runbook test:** a tabletop incident where responders may use only the runbooks.\n- **Handoff test:** the customer's operator performs a rollback from the operations guide before handoff.\n\nEach stall is a bug in the doc. This is the documentation equivalent of a failover drill, and for the\nsame reason: an unexercised path should be presumed broken.\n\n### Writing so it gets used\n- **Lead with the answer,** then the detail.\n- **Make commands copy-pasteable** and state what success looks like.\n- **Write for the reader's state:** an on-call engineer at 2 a.m. needs the first safe action, not the\n  history.","example":"### CI freshness check for documented code\nA CI check that fails the build when code changes but the document that describes it does not. Each doc\ndeclares which paths it covers in a `covers:` line; the check compares last-commit times.\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Fail CI when documented code changed after its doc was last updated.\"\"\"\nimport pathlib, re, subprocess, sys\n\ndef last_commit_ts(path: str) -> int:\n    out = subprocess.run([\"git\", \"log\", \"-1\", \"--format=%ct\", \"--\", path],\n                         capture_output=True, text=True, check=True).stdout.strip()\n    return int(out or 0)                     # 0 if the path has no history\n\nstale = []\nfor doc in pathlib.Path(\"docs\").rglob(\"*.md\"):\n    m = re.search(r\"^covers:\\s*(.+)$\", doc.read_text(), re.M)\n    if not m:                                # e.g. covers: src/extract/ prompts/invoice.yaml\n        continue\n    doc_ts = last_commit_ts(str(doc))\n    for target in m.group(1).split():\n        if last_commit_ts(target) > doc_ts:\n            stale.append(f\"{doc}: '{target}' changed after this doc was last updated\")\n\nprint(\"\\n\".join(stale) or \"all covered docs are fresh\")\nsys.exit(1 if stale else 0)\n```\n\n- **On a customer deployment,** `docs/runbooks/extraction-backlog.md` might cover `src/extract/` and\n  `prompts/invoice.yaml`. When someone edits the invoice prompt, CI fails until the runbook is touched,\n  which forces a reviewer to ask whether its steps and expected outputs still hold.\n- **It is a nudge, not proof:** a trivial edit silences it, so pair it with the pull-request question\n  about invalidated docs.\n- **It needs real history.** CI often checks out a shallow clone (GitHub Actions fetches one commit by\n  default), where paths older than the clone report the same boundary commit and the check silently\n  passes, so fetch full history (or at least the merge base) in the job that runs it.\n\n### Production snippet: a runbook in the fixed order\nOne alert, one page, written for the 2 a.m. reader. Names and thresholds are illustrative.\n\n```markdown\n# Runbook: extraction backlog growing\ncovers: src/extract/ prompts/invoice.yaml\nOwner: customer ops team, document-AI on-call. Review by: next release.\n\nSymptom: alert \"extraction_queue_age_high\" fires; confirm on the queue dashboard\nthat the oldest job is older than 15 minutes and still rising.\n\nUser impact: invoices are not posted to the ERP; nothing is lost, only delayed.\n\nFirst safe action: roll back the last prompt or model change.\n  ./deploy rollback --component extractor --to previous\n  Success looks like: queue age falls within 10 minutes.\n\nDiagnosis (only after stabilising):\n  1. Check provider error rate on the extractor traces; 429s mean quota, see the quota how-to.\n  2. Check the last deploy in the release log; compare system card versions.\n\nStop and escalate if: queue age still rises 15 minutes after rollback,\nor any job shows a partial ERP write. Call the platform on-call (see ops guide).\n```\n\n### Agent system card generated from the tool registry\nThe tool list in the card is rendered from the same definitions the agent sends to the model, so the\ncard cannot drift from what the agent can actually do. The registry shape and names are illustrative.\n\n```python\nTOOLS = [  # the same list the agent loop passes to the model\n    {\"name\": \"search_invoices\", \"description\": \"Find invoices by vendor or number. Read-only.\",\n     \"access\": \"read\", \"system\": \"ERP\", \"approval\": False},\n    {\"name\": \"post_invoice\", \"description\": \"Post one validated invoice to the ERP ledger.\",\n     \"access\": \"write\", \"system\": \"ERP\", \"approval\": True},\n]\nMANIFEST = {\"model\": \"provider-model-2025-01-15\", \"prompt\": \"invoice-agent@v14\",\n            \"index\": \"vendors-2025-02\", \"max_steps\": 12, \"eval_pass_rate\": 0.94}\n\ndef render_card(tools, manifest):\n    lines = [\"# Agent system card: invoice agent\", \"\"]\n    lines += [f\"- {k}: {v}\" for k, v in manifest.items()]   # pulled, never typed\n    lines += [\"\", \"| Tool | Access | System | Human approval |\", \"|---|---|---|---|\"]\n    lines += [f'| {t[\"name\"]} | {t[\"access\"]} | {t[\"system\"]} | {\"yes\" if t[\"approval\"] else \"no\"} |'\n              for t in tools]\n    writes = [t[\"name\"] for t in tools if t[\"access\"] == \"write\" and not t[\"approval\"]]\n    if writes:\n        lines.append(f\"\\nWARNING: unapproved write tools: {', '.join(writes)}\")\n    return \"\\n\".join(lines)\n\nprint(render_card(TOOLS, MANIFEST))\n```\n\n- **Why generate it:** a reviewer asking \"can this agent write to the ERP without a human?\" gets an answer\n  that matches production, *because* the card and the agent read the same list.\n- **Run it in CI:** publish the card per release tag, and turn the warning into a non-zero exit so a\n  write tool without human approval cannot merge unless an ADR records why.","patterns":["**Full-history checkout for doc checks** — when a CI job compares commit times, fetch full history; with GitHub Actions that is `actions/checkout` with `fetch-depth: 0`, since its default fetches only one commit. Monitor the check's failure rate: a check that never fails is probably blind.","**Pull-request template with a docs question** — add \"which docs does this change invalidate?\" as a required checklist item and ask reviewers to block merges that leave it blank. Monitor the share of merged PRs that touched code covered by a doc but not the doc.","**System card rendered from the deployment manifest** — generate the model, prompt and index version lines at build time from the registry or manifest instead of typing them. Monitor that the published card's versions match what production reports.","**Generated reference on every build** — publish API reference from the OpenAPI schema the service already serves, and configuration reference from the config schema. Monitor build warnings for undocumented endpoints or keys.","**Doc drills before handoff** — schedule a tabletop incident and a customer-operator rollback using only the docs, and file each stall as a doc bug with an owner. Monitor stalls per drill and time to find the first safe action.","**Tool descriptions reviewed like prompts** — treat changes to a tool's name, description or parameter docs as behaviour changes: version them with the prompt, run the agent eval set before merging, and regenerate the system card. Monitor tool-selection errors and invalid-argument rates per tool after each change."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent's behaviour lives in artefacts that are not obviously code:\n  model snapshot, system prompt, tool schemas, retrieval index, eval set. When a run goes wrong, the first\n  question is which of those changed, and only versioned docs and registries answer it.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Tool names, descriptions and parameter docs in your tool schemas\n  (the model reads them on every call), an agent system card, runbooks for agent alerts, decision records\n  for loop design, and repository instruction files that coding agents read.\n- **What you control.** One reader per document (human operator or model), docs changed in the same pull\n  request as the prompt or tool, versions pulled from the manifest, and drills that test runbooks.\n- **What you can skip.** Documentation-tool debates and style guides beyond the four Diátaxis types. The\n  mechanism that forces updates at the moment of change is what matters.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Handing an agent to a customer's operations team.** Runbooks for agent alerts, the operations guide\n  and the agent system card let their on-call roll back a prompt or disable a tool without you.\n- **Security and compliance review of an agent.** The data-flow overview and a generated tool and\n  permission table answer \"what can it touch, and who approves writes?\"\n- **Debugging a behaviour change.** Per-release cards with pulled model, prompt, tool and index versions\n  let you diff what ran last week against what runs now.\n- **Improving tool-call accuracy.** Rewriting tool descriptions and parameter docs, then re-running evals,\n  is often cheaper than more prompt engineering.","mini_project":"**Generated agent system card with a freshness gate (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** produce documentation for a small tool-calling agent that cannot drift from what it runs.\n- **Steps:** take an agent with three or four tools (at least one write tool); render a system card from\n  its tool registry and a version manifest, as in the example; write one runbook for \"step cap hit rate\n  rising\" with a `covers:` line; add the freshness check to CI with full-history checkout.\n- **Done when:** adding a tool changes the card automatically, editing the prompt without touching the\n  runbook fails CI, and a teammate can disable a tool using only the runbook."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"Prompt, tool or config change\"] --> PR[\"Pull request\"]\n    PR --> Q{\"Which docs does this invalidate?\"}\n    Q --> CI[\"CI: links, samples, freshness\"]\n    CI -->|\"stale doc\"| U[\"Update doc in same PR\"]\n    U --> CI\n    CI -->|\"pass\"| M[\"Merge and tag release\"]\n    M --> P[\"Publish docs for that version\"]\n    P --> T[\"Drill: newcomer, tabletop, handoff\"]\n    T -->|\"reader stalled\"| U","caption":"The loop that keeps an agent's docs true: every prompt, tool or config change passes the invalidation\nquestion and the freshness check, and every stall found by a drill goes back in as a doc fix."},"interview":[{"q":"Why does documentation go stale even on disciplined teams, and what actually fixes it?","a":"- **The incentive is split:** the author pays to update a doc, while a different person later pays for\n  it being wrong, so drift is the default, not a character flaw.\n- **The fix is a mechanism at the moment of change:** docs in the same repository and pull request, a\n  template question about invalidated docs, and CI freshness checks.\n- **Plus ownership:** every document has a named owner and a review date."},{"q":"Your agent's output quality changed last week and nobody knows why. What documentation would have answered it in minutes?","a":"- **An AI system card** listing the deployed model, prompt and index versions, pulled from the registry\n  or manifest rather than typed.\n- **Per-release tagging** of docs and versions, so you can diff what ran last week against what runs now.\n- **Decision records** that explain why the prompt or retry exists, so nobody \"simplifies\" a workaround."},{"q":"When would you not use docs as code?","a":"- **For customer-facing guides owned by support or product staff,** because they find repositories hard\n  to edit; a wiki with named owners fits better.\n- **For throwaway prototypes,** where long documents are pure maintenance cost.\n- **Keep docs as code** for anything tied to behaviour: runbooks, reference and the system card."},{"q":"How do you know a runbook works before the real incident?","a":"- **Test it with someone other than the author:** a tabletop where responders may use only the runbooks,\n  and a customer operator doing a rollback from the guide before handoff.\n- **Treat each stall as a doc bug** with an owner, because an unexercised path should be presumed broken.\n- **Measure** time to find the first safe action in each drill and in real incident timelines."},{"q":"Your doc freshness check has never failed in CI. What do you suspect?","a":"- **A shallow clone:** CI often checks out few commits (one by default on GitHub Actions), so paths\n  report the same boundary commit and the comparison always passes.\n- **Fix it** by fetching full history or the merge base in that job, then confirm the check fails on a\n  test change to a covered path.\n- **Also check coverage:** docs without a `covers:` line are skipped silently."},{"q":"A security reviewer asks what your agent can do without a human. Which documents answer that, and how do you keep them true?","a":"- **The agent system card:** tools with read or write access, target systems, approval points, step and\n  token caps, and the handoff path.\n- **Decision records** for each write tool explaining why it needs approval or why it does not.\n- **Keep it true by generation:** render the tool table from the same registry the agent loop sends to\n  the model, publish it per release, and fail CI on an unapproved write tool."}],"tradeoffs":["**Docs as code vs editor reach.** It keeps documentation current with changes, but non-engineers find repositories hard to edit; it flips toward a wiki for customer-facing guides owned by support or product staff.","**Generated vs hand-written reference.** Generated reference never drifts from the source, but reads poorly and lacks intent; it flips to hand-written sections where the reader needs context a schema cannot express.","**Full document set vs maintenance.** It eases handoff and audits, but every page is a maintenance liability; prefer fewer, owned documents when the system is still changing weekly.","**Detailed runbooks vs judgement.** They shorten recovery for known failures, but encourage blind command-following; they need a \"stop and escalate if\" line for situations outside what the author anticipated.","**Rich tool descriptions vs context budget.** Detailed descriptions cut wrong tool calls, but every word is sent on every model call and costs tokens; it flips toward terse descriptions plus a reference page when the agent has dozens of tools and the descriptions dominate the prompt."],"pitfalls":["**Mixing tutorial, reference and rationale in one page.** Symptom: readers asking questions in chat that the document technically answers somewhere in its fourth screen.","**Writing documentation once at handoff.** Symptom: the customer's team discovering, during their first incident, that the runbook names a config key that was renamed months ago.","**Documenting what the code does but not why.** Symptom: a new owner \"simplifying\" a retry or a prompt instruction that existed to work around a specific provider failure.","**Omitting model and prompt versions from system documentation.** Symptom: a behaviour change nobody can attribute because no one knows what was running last month.","**Only ever reviewing docs by reading them.** Symptom: the first real use revealing that a runbook assumes access, tools or tribal context the customer's operator does not have.","**Leaving documents without owners.** Symptom: a wiki where every page has a \"this may be out of date\" banner and nobody trusts any of it.","**Hand-maintaining the agent's tool list in the docs.** Symptom: a security review approves an agent whose card omits a write tool added last sprint. Fix: render the tool table from the registry.","**Vague tool descriptions.** Symptom: the agent calls the wrong tool or passes malformed arguments, and the team tunes the system prompt instead. Fix: rewrite the description and parameter docs, then re-run evals."],"when":"- **Use when:** someone other than the current author must operate, review, extend or approve the\n  agent, which for delivered work is always; tool descriptions are needed from the first prototype,\n  because the model reads them.\n- **Start early** with the architecture overview and decision records, *because* security reviews and\n  design debates need them; write runbooks with the alerts they serve; write the handoff and system\n  card before the people who know the answers leave.\n- **Avoid when:** the code is a throwaway prototype; do not write long documents for it.\n- **Measure:** the questions that stop arriving, and incident timelines: how long did responders spend\n  finding what to do?\n- **Interviews:** describing how you kept documentation true, not merely that you wrote it, signals real\n  ownership experience.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Updates docs in the same pull request as the change, keeps reference generated from schemas, and writes runbooks that start with the first safe action.","architect":"Owns the architecture overview and decision records, and sets the documentation standard every delivered system must meet before it is considered production-ready.","fde":"Writes the handoff set the customer's operators will actually use, and the data-flow and system card pages their security and compliance reviewers ask for first."},"check":{"q":"You are handing an AI document-extraction system to a customer's operations team in three weeks. What\ndocumentation do you produce, in what order, and how do you keep it accurate after you leave?","a":"First the architecture overview with the data flow and trust boundaries, because their security\nreview gates go-live. Then runbooks for each alert, written from real incidents in the pilot, then the\noperations guide with ownership, escalation and rollback. Next an AI system card listing model and\nprompt versions, evaluation results, intended use and known failure modes, plus generated configuration\nreference. Decision records for the major choices go in alongside. To keep it true, it lives in their\nrepository, each doc has a named owner on their side, the pull-request template asks which docs a change\ninvalidates, and CI checks links and freshness. I would run one tabletop incident using only the docs."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why separate how-to guides from tutorials instead of writing one combined guide?","options":["Tutorials must be generated automatically while how-to guides are written by hand","A newcomer needs a single guaranteed path to learn, while a competent reader needs steps for a specific goal","How-to guides are only for internal staff and tutorials only for customers","Combined guides cannot include code samples"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Readers arrive in different modes. A tutorial removes choices to build confidence; a how-to assumes competence and targets a goal. Mixing them gives the learner too many options and the expert too much hand-holding."},{"q":"Which artefact best answers \"why did we choose this retrieval design over fine-tuning?\" a year later?","options":["The API reference generated from the OpenAPI schema","The runbook for the retrieval latency alert","An architecture decision record capturing context, options considered and consequences","The commit message of the first retrieval commit"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Decision records exist to preserve rationale and rejected alternatives. Reference and runbooks describe what and how, and commit messages are scattered and rarely capture the options weighed."},{"q":"What is the strongest reason to keep documentation in the same repository as the code?","options":["Repositories render Markdown better than wikis do","It removes the need for document owners","Docs can be changed and reviewed in the same pull request that changes the behaviour they describe","It makes the documentation publicly visible"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Staleness is cheapest to catch at the moment of change. Co-locating docs lets review and CI tie them to the code change; ownership is still needed, and visibility depends on repository settings."}]},{"key":"delivery-ownership/productizing-repeated-customer-solutions","sources":[],"why":"- **The first build is bespoke on purpose.** A forward-deployed engineer is paid to make one customer\n  successful fast: their connector, their prompt, their output format.\n- **Copying is the fast path, and it compounds.** The second customer asks for \"the same thing\", so the\n  repository gets copied and changed. By the fifth customer there are five forks:\n  - a security fix has to be applied five times;\n  - a model upgrade means re-tuning and re-evaluating five prompts that have drifted apart;\n  - every improvement made for one customer is unavailable to the others;\n  - the engineers who know each fork become permanent bottlenecks.\n- **That is the signature of a services business.** Delivery cost per customer stops falling, turning a\n  product company into a consultancy.\n- **The naive opposite fails too.** Building a general platform after the first customer means guessing\n  which parts will vary. With one example you cannot tell an essential requirement from an accident of\n  that customer's environment, so you get knobs nobody uses and no seam where the second customer\n  actually differs.\n- **Productizing finds the moment in between,** with enough repetition to see the real shape, early\n  enough that forks have not diverged beyond recovery. It is also a feedback channel: the field sees\n  demand before the product team does, and productizing turns that evidence into roadmap.","how":"### 1. Detect repetition\n- **Keep an inventory** of every deployment and what each component does.\n- **Rule of three:** build the first, copy the second while recording every difference, extract on the\n  third, *because* with two instances you cannot tell which differences will recur, while a third\n  usually shows what genuinely varies and what was coincidence.\n- **Three is a heuristic, not a law:** a well-understood domain can justify extracting at two, and a\n  very heterogeneous one may need more.\n\n### 2. Run the economics, not just the count\nEach recurring change (model upgrade, security fix, shared bug fix) costs:\n\n| Shape | Cost per recurring change |\n|---|---|\n| N forks | `N × c_fork` |\n| Product | `c_core + N × c_tenant`, where `c_tenant` is running and reviewing one customer's regression suite |\n| Saving per change | `N × (c_fork − c_tenant) − c_core` |\n\n- **Break-even:** extraction pays back when the per-change saving, summed over the changes you expect in\n  your planning horizon, exceeds the one-time cost of building the core and migrating the forks.\n- **What the formula shows:** the saving grows linearly with customer count, and it is negative for\n  small N if the core is expensive to change, which is the quantitative form of \"too early\".\n\n### 3. Classify every difference\nDiff the instances component by component and put each difference in one bucket:\n\n| Bucket | What it is | Where it goes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Configuration** | differences in data, not logic: field lists, thresholds, clause taxonomies, prompt variables, few-shot examples, output destinations | a validated, versioned config schema |\n| **Extension** | customer-specific logic that cannot be data, such as a connector to a proprietary system | behind a defined interface, *because* it lets the core evolve without breaking them |\n| **Accident** | no reason beyond who wrote it and when | pick one behaviour and converge |\n| **Genuine one-off** | a requirement no other customer shares | outside the product as paid services work, *because* pulling it into the core taxes every other customer |\n\n### 4. Build the core and its seams\n- **The core owns everything shared:** orchestration, the prompt template, model routing and version\n  pinning, evaluation harness, observability, security controls.\n- **One prompt template with slots,** not N forked prompts, so a model upgrade is tuned once.\n- **Evaluation splits** into a shared core suite plus a per-customer regression suite, *because* the\n  core must prove it did not break anyone in particular.\n- **Configuration is now code that ships.** A tenant's config is validated against the schema on load\n  (bad config fails at deploy, not at 2 a.m. on the first document), versioned, reviewed, and gated by\n  that tenant's regression suite, *because* changing a clause description changes prompt text, and\n  prompt text changes behaviour as surely as a code change does.\n\n### What agents add to the core\n| Agent part | Shared core | Per-tenant |\n|---|---|---|\n| System prompt | one template, tuned per model upgrade | slot values: tone, categories, policies, few-shot examples |\n| Tools | registry, schemas, timeouts, idempotency, audit | allowlist of enabled tools, approval rules, credentials |\n| Connectors | tool interface and error handling | extension tool for a proprietary system |\n| Memory and retrieval | storage, retrieval code, isolation checks | tenant-scoped namespace or index |\n| Loop limits | defaults and hard maximums | step cap and budget within those maximums |\n| Evals | core suite for loop behaviour | regression suite from the tenant's real tasks |\n\n- **Tenant isolation is part of the core,** *because* a shared agent that can read another tenant's\n  memory or call another tenant's tools is a security incident, not a bug.\n- **Enabled tools are config, tool code is not.** Turning a tool on for a tenant is a reviewed config\n  change gated by that tenant's suite; writing a new tool is an extension.\n\n### 5. Migrate the forks\n1. **Move existing customers onto the product, least-divergent first.**\n2. **Gate each move** on their regression suite showing parity with the fork's behaviour.\n3. **Finish the job.** Stopping halfway is the worst state: you now maintain a product and forks.\n4. **Set a sunset date** after which forks receive security fixes only.\n\n### 6. Transfer ownership\n- **A product team owns the core's** roadmap, versioning and deprecation.\n- **Upgrade policy:** every tenant moves to a new core release within a stated window, *because* letting\n  each customer pin a version indefinitely recreates the forks in time instead of in code.\n- **Field engineers** consume the core, contribute extensions, and file evidence-backed requests.\n- **Track:** time to onboard a new customer, the share of each deployment that is configuration rather\n  than custom code, and engineering hours per customer per month.","example":"### Contract-review pipelines: diff three forks into one config\nThree customers run contract-review pipelines built by the field team:\n\n| Customer | Source | Sink |\n|---|---|---|\n| A | SharePoint | Salesforce |\n| B | Box | Jira |\n| C | S3 | a webhook |\n\n- **Extensions:** the ingestion connectors and output sinks.\n- **Configuration:** the clause types, risk threshold and few-shot examples.\n- **Accident:** two different chunking strategies.\n- **Genuine one-off:** C's requirement to score clauses against its internal playbook.\n\nThe resulting customer config:\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Literal, Protocol\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field\n\nclass Connector(Protocol):                 # extension point: custom code lives behind this\n    def fetch(self, since: str) -> list[bytes]: ...\n\nclass ClauseType(BaseModel):\n    name: str\n    description: str                       # injected into the shared extraction prompt template\n    examples: list[str] = Field(default_factory=list, max_length=5)\n\nclass CustomerConfig(BaseModel):           # configuration: data, validated, versioned per tenant\n    schema_version: Literal[2]\n    tenant_id: str\n    source: Literal[\"sharepoint\", \"box\", \"s3\", \"custom\"]\n    clause_types: list[ClauseType]\n    risk_threshold: float = Field(0.7, ge=0.0, le=1.0)\n    sink: Literal[\"salesforce\", \"jira\", \"webhook\"]\n    model_alias: str = \"contract-extraction-default\"   # pinned and upgraded centrally\n```\n\nThe fourth customer then needs a connector and a config file, not a new codebase.\n\n### Break-even arithmetic for the extraction\nWith illustrative effort figures: a model upgrade costs about 40 engineer-hours per fork to re-tune and\nre-evaluate; on the product, 50 hours on the core plus 4 hours per customer to run and review its\nregression suite; building the core and migrating the forks costs 600 hours (also illustrative).\n\n```python\ndef per_change(n, c_fork, c_core, c_tenant):\n    forks = n * c_fork\n    product = c_core + n * c_tenant\n    return forks, product, forks - product      # saving = N*(c_fork - c_tenant) - c_core\n\ndef changes_to_pay_back(build_cost, saving):\n    return float(\"inf\") if saving <= 0 else build_cost / saving\n\nC_FORK, C_CORE, C_TENANT, BUILD = 40, 50, 4, 600   # engineer-hours, illustrative\n\nfor n in (1, 2, 3, 10):\n    forks, product, saving = per_change(n, C_FORK, C_CORE, C_TENANT)\n    print(f\"N={n:>2}: forks {forks:>3} h, product {product:>3} h, \"\n          f\"saving {saving:>4} h, pays back after {changes_to_pay_back(BUILD, saving):.1f} changes\")\n# N= 1: forks  40 h, product  54 h, saving  -14 h, pays back after inf changes\n# N= 2: forks  80 h, product  58 h, saving   22 h, pays back after 27.3 changes\n# N= 3: forks 120 h, product  62 h, saving   58 h, pays back after 10.3 changes\n# N=10: forks 400 h, product  90 h, saving  310 h, pays back after 1.9 changes\n```\n\n| Customers | Forks per upgrade | Product per upgrade | Saving | Upgrades to repay 600 hours |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 3 | 120 hours | 62 hours | 58 hours | about ten |\n| 10 | 400 hours | 90 hours | 310 hours | about two |\n\n- **Count every recurring change.** Security and bug fixes shorten both payback periods, which is why\n  the honest case is made with all recurring changes, not one.\n- **Too early is visible:** at one customer the saving is negative, so no number of upgrades pays back.\n\n### Agent scenario: one support-triage agent core, many tenants\nThe support-triage forks from Check yourself become one agent core. Each tenant gets a config that\nfills prompt slots and picks tools from the shared registry; the escalation rule engine becomes an\nextension tool only the tenant that needs it enables.\n\n```python\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field, model_validator\n\nREGISTRY = {\"search_kb\": \"read\", \"create_ticket\": \"write\", \"route_to_queue\": \"write\",\n            \"run_escalation_rules\": \"write\"}           # tools the core implements (or extensions)\n\nclass AgentTenantConfig(BaseModel):\n    tenant_id: str\n    categories: list[str]                              # prompt slot\n    tone: str = \"concise and polite\"                   # prompt slot\n    queue_map: dict[str, str]                          # category -> destination queue\n    enabled_tools: list[str]\n    approval_required: list[str] = Field(default_factory=list)\n    max_steps: int = Field(8, ge=1, le=15)             # core sets the hard maximum\n    memory_namespace: str                              # tenant-scoped memory and retrieval\n\n    @model_validator(mode=\"after\")\n    def check_tools(self):\n        unknown = set(self.enabled_tools) - REGISTRY.keys()\n        if unknown:\n            raise ValueError(f\"unknown tools: {sorted(unknown)}\")\n        if set(self.approval_required) - set(self.enabled_tools):\n            raise ValueError(\"approval rules name tools that are not enabled\")\n        # exact prefix plus separator, so tenant \"acme\" cannot claim \"acme2:...\"\n        if not self.memory_namespace.startswith(f\"{self.tenant_id}:\"):\n            raise ValueError(\"memory namespace must be scoped to the tenant\")\n        return self\n```\n\n- **Why the separator matters:** a bare `startswith(tenant_id)` check would let tenant `acme` load\n  `acme2:tickets`, which is exactly the cross-tenant read the core must refuse.\n\n| Change | With 4 agent forks | With one core and 4 configs |\n|---|---|---|\n| Model upgrade | re-tune 4 prompts and tool descriptions, 4 eval runs | tune 1 template, run 4 tenant suites |\n| New tool for everyone | implement 4 times | add to registry, enable per tenant |\n| Fifth customer | a fifth fork | a config file, plus an extension only if truly unique |","patterns":["**Validate tenant config at deploy time** — when configs live in a repository, load each one with the schema in CI (with Pydantic v2, `CustomerConfig.model_validate` on the parsed file) so a bad threshold or unknown sink fails the pipeline, not the first document. Monitor config validation failures per tenant.","**Per-tenant regression gate on core releases** — run every tenant's regression suite against a core release candidate and block promotion if any tenant falls below its baseline. Monitor pass rate per tenant per release and how often one tenant blocks a release.","**Canary tenants for core rollouts** — roll a new core release to one or two low-risk tenants first, then the rest within the upgrade window. Monitor error rate and quality scores on canary tenants against the previous release.","**Fork sunset with a hard date** — publish the date after which forks get security fixes only, and track remaining forks on the delivery dashboard. Monitor the number of live forks and hours spent backporting.","**Configuration share as a delivery metric** — for each deployment, measure how much is configuration and extensions versus custom code changes to the core. Monitor time to onboard and engineering hours per customer per month as the count grows.","**Per-tenant tool allowlists and namespaces** — load the enabled tools and the memory or index namespace from tenant config at run start, and have the core refuse any tool call or retrieval outside them. Run a cross-tenant isolation test in every release. Monitor refused out-of-scope calls per tenant; any non-zero count deserves a look."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agent forks drift fast: each customer's system prompt, tool set and\n  few-shot examples get tuned separately, so a model upgrade means re-tuning and re-evaluating N prompts\n  and N tool lists. A shared core lets you upgrade, fix and evaluate the agent once.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** One prompt template with slots, a tool registry with per-tenant\n  allowlists, connectors exposed as tools behind one interface, tenant-scoped memory and retrieval\n  indexes, model aliases routed centrally, and per-tenant regression suites in CI.\n- **What you control.** What is tenant config (prompt variables, enabled tools, thresholds, step caps)\n  versus extension code (a proprietary connector tool), the upgrade window, and the release gate.\n- **What you can skip.** Platform-strategy theory and pricing models. The rule of three, the difference\n  buckets and the break-even arithmetic are what you use.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Forward-deployed agent teams.** The third support, contract-review or research agent built for a\n  customer is the signal to extract a shared agent core.\n- **Model upgrades across many tenants.** One prompt template and per-tenant suites turn a model swap\n  from N re-tuning projects into one tuning pass plus N automated gates.\n- **Multi-tenant agent platforms.** Tool allowlists, tenant-scoped memory and config validation let a new\n  customer onboard with a config file instead of a code change.\n- **Deciding what to refuse.** The difference buckets give the field a principled answer when one\n  customer asks for logic nobody else needs.","mini_project":"**Collapse three agent forks into one core (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** replace three copies of a small tool-calling agent with one core plus three tenant configs.\n- **Steps:** write three variants of a triage agent that differ in categories, tone, enabled tools and\n  one custom tool; classify every difference into the four buckets; build the config model with a tool\n  allowlist and memory namespace check; write five regression tasks per tenant and run them against both\n  the forks and the core.\n- **Done when:** all three tenants pass their suites on the core, a fourth tenant onboards with only a\n  config file, and a cross-tenant tool call or memory read is refused by the core."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    D[\"Difference between agent forks\"] --> Q1{\"Any reason it differs?\"}\n    Q1 -->|\"no\"| A[\"Accident: converge on one\"]\n    Q1 -->|\"yes\"| Q2{\"Expressible as data?\"}\n    Q2 -->|\"yes\"| C[\"Configuration: versioned schema\"]\n    Q2 -->|\"no\"| Q3{\"Fits a shared seam or recurs?\"}\n    Q3 -->|\"yes\"| E[\"Extension behind interface\"]\n    Q3 -->|\"no\"| O[\"One-off: paid services work\"]\n    C --> G[\"Gate: tenant regression suite\"]\n    E --> G\n    G --> P[\"Shared core release\"]","caption":"The decision path for each difference found when diffing agent forks (prompt, tools, connectors,\nlimits). Only configuration and extensions enter the product; one-offs stay as priced services work.\nA proprietary connector is an extension even for one customer, because it plugs into an existing seam."},"interview":[{"q":"What does \"productizing\" a field solution mean, and why not just keep copying the repository?","a":"- **It means one shared core,** per-customer configuration and defined extension points, instead of a\n  fork per customer.\n- **Copying fails at scale** because every security fix and model upgrade must be applied N times to\n  prompts that have drifted apart, so delivery cost per customer stops falling.\n- **The test of success** is onboarding time and per-customer maintenance hours falling as the customer\n  count grows."},{"q":"You are diffing three forks. How do you decide what becomes configuration, what becomes an extension, and what you refuse?","a":"- **Configuration** is any difference expressible as data: thresholds, taxonomies, prompt variables,\n  few-shot examples, destinations.\n- **Extension** is logic that cannot be data, such as a proprietary connector, placed behind an interface\n  so the core can evolve.\n- **Accidents converge; one-offs stay out** as priced services work, because pulling them into the core\n  taxes every other customer."},{"q":"When would you productize at two customers, and when would you wait past three?","a":"- **Extract at two** when the domain is well understood and you can already tell essential variation\n  from accident.\n- **Wait longer** when the domain is heterogeneous and each customer still changes your picture of the\n  shape.\n- **Let the economics decide:** the per-change saving is negative for small N when the core is expensive\n  to change."},{"q":"Walk me through the break-even for extracting a core across ten forks.","a":"- **Per recurring change:** forks cost `N × c_fork`, the product costs `c_core + N × c_tenant`.\n- **With illustrative numbers** of 40 hours per fork, 50 on the core and 4 per tenant, ten customers save\n  310 hours per upgrade, so a 600-hour extraction repays in about two upgrades.\n- **Then add security and bug fixes,** which shorten the payback further."},{"q":"After productizing your agent, a core release silently broke one customer. What went wrong and what do you change?","a":"- **Likely missing or weak per-customer regression suites,** so the shared core suite passed while that\n  tenant's edge cases failed.\n- **Change the release gate:** every tenant's suite runs against the release candidate, and canary\n  tenants get it first.\n- **Check for a forked prompt or a config change** that bypassed review, since prompt text changes\n  behaviour like code."},{"q":"Three customers run forked copies of your agent with different tool sets. How do you turn them into one agent core?","a":"- **Diff the forks by agent part:** prompt text, tool list, tool descriptions, connectors, loop limits and\n  memory; classify each difference as configuration, extension, accident or one-off.\n- **Build the core:** one prompt template with slots, a tool registry with per-tenant allowlists, tenant\n  scoped memory, and hard limits the config can only lower.\n- **Migrate with evidence:** each tenant's regression suite from its real tasks must show parity before\n  its fork is retired."}],"tradeoffs":["**Early productizing vs the wrong seam.** It avoids fork divergence, but with fewer than three examples you will likely abstract the wrong seam; it flips toward early extraction only when the domain is already well understood.","**Rich configuration vs test matrix.** A rich schema lets customers onboard without code, but every option multiplies the test matrix; prefer an extension point once options start interacting with each other.","**Full migration vs effort now.** Migrating every fork onto the core cuts long-term maintenance, but costs revalidation effort and customer goodwill now; leave a fork in place only if that customer is winding down.","**Refusing one-offs vs strategic deals.** Refusing keeps the core clean, but can lose a strategic deal; accept them as services work outside the core, priced to cover their ongoing maintenance.","**Per-tenant prompt freedom vs upgradeability.** Letting tenants edit free-form instructions fits each customer better, but every free-form block must be re-evaluated on model upgrades; it flips toward structured slots once more than a few tenants share the agent."],"pitfalls":["**Abstracting from a single deployment.** Symptom: the second customer needing changes inside the \"generic\" core rather than in its configuration.","**Letting configuration become a programming language.** Symptom: config files with conditionals and expressions that only the original engineer can safely edit.","**Stopping the migration halfway.** Symptom: the team maintaining the product plus most of the old forks, with fixes landing in one and not the others.","**Forking the shared prompt per customer \"just for now\".** Symptom: a model upgrade that passes core evals but regresses two customers whose prompt copies nobody re-tested.","**Letting tenants pin core versions with no upgrade window.** Symptom: production running several core releases at once, with each fix backported by hand, which is the fork problem again.","**Productizing without per-customer regression suites.** Symptom: a core release that silently breaks one customer's edge cases, discovered from their support ticket.","**Sharing agent memory or indexes across tenants.** Symptom: an agent quoting another customer's ticket or document. Fix: tenant-scoped namespaces enforced by the core, with an isolation test per release.","**Putting tenant branches inside tool code.** Symptom: `if tenant == ...` checks spreading through shared tools. Fix: move the difference into config or a separate extension tool."],"when":"- **Use when:** the same agent shape (prompt, tools, loop) appears for the third customer, onboarding\n  time per customer has stopped falling, or a model upgrade or security fix has to be applied in\n  several places.\n- **Avoid when:** the domain is still being discovered and each customer teaches you something new about\n  the shape.\n- **Before committing:** run the break-even arithmetic with your own hour estimates for recurring\n  changes and for the extraction itself.\n- **Measure:** onboarding time, custom code per deployment and maintenance hours per customer, before\n  and after.\n- **Field habit:** record every difference you introduce when copying a solution; it is what makes\n  extraction possible later.\n- **Interviews:** describe how you decided what became configuration, what became an extension, and\n  what you refused.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the config schema, the extension interfaces and the per-customer regression suites that prove each migration preserved the behaviour the customer relied on.","architect":"Decides where the seams go, which differences become configuration versus extension, and when a one-off is refused from the core to protect every other customer.","fde":"Spots the repetition first, documents how each deployment diverges, and brings the evidence to the product team instead of quietly maintaining another fork."},"check":{"q":"Four customers run forks of your support-triage agent. One adds a custom escalation rule engine; the\nother three differ only in categories, tone and destination queue. A new customer signs next month. What do you do?","a":"The three similar forks show the shape: categories, tone guidance and queue mapping become a\nvalidated config schema, the prompt becomes one template with slots, and destinations become sink\nextensions behind an interface. I would build the core with a shared eval suite, write regression suites\nfrom each customer's real tickets, and migrate the least-divergent fork first. The rule engine is either an\nextension point, if others are likely to need rules, or a supported one-off outside the core. The new\ncustomer onboards onto the product, not a fifth fork, which is the first proof the extraction worked."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is the third instance, rather than the first, the usual point to extract a product?","options":["Because contracts typically forbid reusing code before three customers","Because several instances are needed to tell essential variation from accidents of one environment","Because model providers discount usage after three tenants","Because the first two builds are always discarded"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With one example every detail looks essential. Comparing three shows which differences recur and belong in configuration and which were incidental, so the abstraction fits real demand."},{"q":"A customer needs a connector to a proprietary document system nobody else uses. Where should it live?","options":["Inside the core pipeline, behind a customer-ID conditional","In a new fork of the whole product for that customer","Behind a defined extension interface, implemented outside the core","As new keys in the shared configuration schema"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Logic that cannot be expressed as data belongs behind an interface so the core can evolve without it. Customer-ID conditionals and forks recreate exactly the divergence productizing removes."},{"q":"What best indicates productizing is working?","options":["The product exposes more configuration options than any single fork had","Every customer request is accepted into the core","The field team stops talking to customers about requirements","Onboarding time and per-customer maintenance hours fall as the customer count grows"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The point is that delivery cost per customer declines. More options or accepting everything usually raises cost, and field feedback is the input productizing depends on."}]}]});
